#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
spec2thunk -- generate an AMD64 "Wine builtin" thunk PE DLL.

The generated DLL is never executed by the host CPU; it exists so that an
x86-64 Windows PE guest can bind its imports to AMD64 code at a known,
tabulated address.  A host-side dispatcher maps a trapping RIP back to an
export index using the exported __wine_thunk_info structure:

    struct {
        uint32_t version;    /* = 4 */
        uint32_t count;
        uint32_t stubs_rva;
        uint32_t stride;     /* = 16 */
        uint32_t names_rva;  /* count x uint32 RVA -> NUL-terminated name  */
        uint32_t sigs_rva;   /* count x uint32 signature descriptor        */
        uint32_t trap_off;   /* byte offset of the trapping instruction
                                within a stub: 3 for trap, 0 for direct    */
        uint32_t impl_names_rva; /* count x uint32 RVA -> the name to resolve
                                in the NATIVE module.  The same string as
                                names_rva[i] except for a variadic, where it
                                is the v-variant -- see below.             */
    };

Version 4 appended impl_names_rva; the first seven fields keep their v3
offsets, so a v3 reader that stops after trap_off still reads the same bytes.

A trapping RIP is stubs_rva + index*stride + trap_off -- NOT a clean multiple
of the stride -- so the dispatcher can both recover the index and confirm the
trap landed exactly on a published trap site.

GUEST ARGUMENT REGISTERS AT THE TRAP.  x86-64 SYSCALL architecturally writes
the address of the following instruction into RCX and RFLAGS into R11.  RCX is
MS-x64's FIRST argument register, so a bare `0f 05` stub destroys arg0 before
the host can see it.  The trap stub therefore rescues it first:

        49 89 ca    mov r10, rcx      ; arg0 out of the way
        0f 05       syscall           ; now free to clobber rcx/r11

which puts the host-visible argument registers at:

        arg0 -> R10 (rescued from RCX)   arg1 -> RDX
        arg2 -> R8                       arg3 -> R9
        arg4+ -> [RSP + 8 + i*8]  (past the return address and the 32-byte
                                   shadow space)

Signature descriptor bits:
    0..7    number of integer/pointer arguments (0..16).  For a VARIADIC this
            is the number of FIXED arguments, i.e. where the `...` begins.
    8       set if the function returns void
    9       VARIADIC -- see below
    10..31  reserved, always 0

SIGNATURES COME FROM WINE'S HEADERS, NOT FROM THE SPEC FILE.  The spec file's
nargs / return-kind / header:line columns are ASSERTIONS: they are checked
against what clang reads out of Wine's real headers (see wine_sig.py) and any
mismatch is a hard failure.  A function whose shape is not representable --
float or vector arguments, struct by value, a return wider than 64 bits, or no
declaration at all -- is REFUSED and listed, never described with a
plausible-looking descriptor.

A va_list ARGUMENT IS NOT REFUSED on this ABI pair -- it is one pointer-sized
slot on both sides, measured, not assumed.  See the long note in wine_sig.py
before changing that back.

TRUE `...` VARIADICS ARE SUPPORTED, VIA THE CALLEE'S OWN v-VARIANT.  A
fixed-arity stub cannot forward `...` -- it cannot know the argument count.
It does not have to: it never calls the variadic function at all.  The host
calls that function's v-variant (printf -> vprintf, _snprintf -> _vsnprintf),
handing it a va_list synthesised from the guest's argument frame starting at
slot nfixed.  That is sound only because ppc64le ELFv2 va_list is a plain
pointer into a flat 8-byte-slot parameter save area, structurally identical to
MS-x64's -- measured end to end on the AC922, a guest-built MS-x64 va_list
consumed correctly by native ppc64 _vsnprintf ("%d-%s-%d" -> "4242-AB-77").
The sibling project wine-spec-thunk refuses va_list
outright; that refusal is about a different boundary and must not be
copied back here.  impl_names_rva[i] is what carries the v-variant name to the
host, and the generator REFUSES rather than guesses: the .thunks line must
name the v-variant, clang must agree the export really is variadic, and the
oracle must confirm nargs(v-variant) == nfixed + 1.  Nothing is derived by
string munging the export name.

Spec file format (see kernel32.thunks):
    DLL   kernel32.dll
    <ExportName>  <imm32>  <nargs>  <int|void>  <header.h>:<line>
    <ExportName>  <imm32>  <nfixed> <int|void>  <header.h>:<line>  variadic=<impl>
Export order in the file IS the stub index order.  The optional sixth token is
the ONLY way to describe a variadic; five tokens keep their exact old meaning.

The location column may name a Wine `.spec` file instead of a header, for the
handful of exports Wine implements in C and exports from a .spec but declares
in NO header (kernel32's delay-load entry points).  That path is a deliberate
downgrade: a .spec pins the arity and the argument classes but carries no
return type, so the <int|void> column becomes an unchecked human assertion and
the entry is listed under a loud banner at generation time.  Use a header
whenever one exists.

BUILDING.  A module with a <name>.thunks file beside its <name>.spec gets an
x86_64-windows/<name>.dll target, and "make" produces it like any other PE
module -- see output_source_thunks() in tools/makedep.c.  "make install" puts
it in $(libdir)/wine/x86_64-windows, which is already where the loader looks
for a builtin of that machine, so nothing needs staging into a prefix by hand.

RUNNING IT DIRECTLY, to regenerate or extend one module after Wine is built or
installed, without a full rebuild:

    tools/spec2thunk/spec2thunk --spec dlls/kernel32/kernel32.thunks \
        --body=trap --out <builddir>/dlls/kernel32/x86_64-windows/kernel32.dll

From the top of the build directory that is enough.  From anywhere else add
--wine-generated <builddir>/include: the widl-generated headers the signature
oracle needs are build output, not source, and the tool says so rather than
letting clang fail confusingly.

THE EXPORT LIST COMES FROM THE MODULE'S OWN WINE .spec.  A .thunks file used to
BE the list, plus an assertion about each signature.  But the list already
exists and nobody maintains it twice: winebuild turns <mod>.spec into the
module's real export table, so it IS, by construction, the set of names a guest
could import.

    spec2thunk --from-spec dlls/kernel32/kernel32.spec --body=trap --out ...

`@ stub` lines are skipped -- there is no implementation to call -- as are
entries this machine does not export at all (-arch=/-i386), -noname entries
(exported by ordinal, so there is no name to bind) and -register entries (which
take a CONTEXT, not an argument list).  Each of those is reported as a SKIP with
its reason, separately from the refusals, because they are not the same thing.

THE .spec SUPPLIES ONLY *WHICH NAMES EXIST*.  Every signature still comes from
clang reading Wine's real headers: a .spec has no return type at all and its
`long` is 32 bits.  What it does state exactly is the ARITY and the argument
CLASSES, and that is used as an independent CROSS-CHECK -- an export whose .spec
arity disagrees with its header declaration is refused and listed, never emitted
on one source's word.  (That check found two real Wine bugs the first time it
ran: kernel32.spec's RtlUnwindEx was one argument short of ntdll's, and
kernel32/kernelbase spelled VerSetConditionMask's ULONGLONG as two `long`s,
which is right on i386 and wrong on win64.  Both fixed.)

THE .spec ALSO SUPPLIES *WHICH ORDINAL EACH NAME ANSWERS TO*, and so does a
.def.  A guest may import by ordinal and carry no name at all -- Steam's
d3ddriverquery64.exe imports d3d12.dll ordinal 101 and nothing else -- so the
thunk's export table has to be numbered the way the real module's is, not the
way the linker feels like numbering it.  See the ORDINALS banner further down
for how that number is derived and why the gaps are left as gaps.

A .thunks FILE IS NOW UNNECESSARY, NOT FORBIDDEN.  A module with nothing to say
needs no file at all.  A module with a few things to say keeps a small one:

    DLL       kernel32.dll
    FROM-SPEC auto                  # or a path; `auto` = <mod>.spec beside it
    EXCLUDE   SomeExport            # drop one name
    DelayLoadFailureHook 0xD1A0 2 int kernel32.spec:366   # an OVERRIDE

With a FROM-SPEC line the export LINES become overrides for the handful of names
the .spec cannot describe -- a `variadic=<v-variant>` opt-in, a .spec location
for something Wine declares in no header, a deliberate arity/return assertion --
and everything else comes from the .spec.  Without one, the file means exactly
what it always meant, which is why the five hand-written modules still build
byte-for-byte identically.

REFUSALS ARE NORMAL AT THIS SCALE AND ARE NOT ERRORS.  A whole kernel32.spec
emits about 87% of its eligible exports; the rest are refused, biggest reason
first, with `--report FILE` giving the per-export detail as TSV.  --strict still
turns any refusal into a failure for a module that expects none.

"""

import argparse
import os
import shutil
import tempfile
import re
import struct
import subprocess
import sys
import time

import wine_sig
from wine_sig import Refused

STRIDE = 16
INFO_VERSION = 7

# Exports this generator makes up, past the end of the module's ordinal space.
# A .thunks or .spec file that names one of them is refused, because the two
# would then be the same name meaning two things.  spec2thunk-check knows the
# same list and excludes it from the "every exported name is in the list"
# checks.
RESERVED_EXPORTS = ('__wine_thunk_info', '__wine_com_thunk_info',
                    '__wine_thunk_qpc')
BUILTIN_SIG = b"Wine builtin DLL\x00"   # 17 bytes, must sit at file offset 0x40
SIG_OFF = 0x40

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TWO GUEST MACHINES
#
# A .thunks file describes a module's exports, not a word size, so the same
# file produces BOTH guest halves: an x86-64 PE for a 64-bit guest and an
# i386 PE for a 32-bit one.  Wine serves whichever the loading image needs
# (get_machine_system_dir()), and a port of Wine owes both -- a 32-bit guest
# that finds no i386 half of a module this tree replaces has no fallback,
# because the replacement IS the implementation.
#
# What differs, and nothing else does:
#
#   trap body   x86-64 needs `mov r10, rcx` first because SYSCALL clobbers
#               RCX, which is MS-x64 argument 0.  i386 passes every argument
#               on the stack, so the stub is the bare trap and trap_off is 0.
#               `int 0x80` is the 32-bit instruction this FEXCore build
#               routes into the same OS_GENERIC sink `0F 05` reaches on the
#               64-bit side, EIP left at the trapping byte -- see the "32-bit
#               (WoW64) lane" banner in dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c.
#   image base  a 32-bit image must load below 4 GiB.
#   toolchain   clang target and lld-link /machine.
#
# The info structures, the stride, the ordinal assignment, the signature
# oracle and every descriptor table are machine-independent by construction:
# they describe the module's C surface, which is the same surface either
# guest calls.  The ABI difference lives entirely in the host dispatcher,
# which reads those same descriptors (see libs/winecom's dispatch32 and
# ntdll's guest thunk dispatcher).
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
MACHINES = {
    'x86_64': dict(
        clang_target='x86_64-windows-gnu',
        link_machine='x64',
        image_base=0x180000000,
        pe_dir='x86_64-windows',
        # mov r10, rcx ; syscall
        trap_body=b'\x49\x89\xca\x0f\x05',
        trap_off=3,
    ),
    'i386': dict(
        clang_target='i386-windows-gnu',
        link_machine='x86',
        image_base=0x10000000,
        pe_dir='i386-windows',
        # int 0x80
        trap_body=b'\xcd\x80',
        trap_off=0,
        # x86 lld-link defaults to /safeseh, which requires every object to
        # carry a registered handler table.  This module is a hand-written
        # stub array with no exception handlers of any kind -- an empty
        # table would be a claim, and the honest statement is that the
        # image opts out.  A guest's own SEH is its business and does not
        # route through here.
        link_extra=['/safeseh:no'],
    ),
}

# Byte offset of the trapping instruction inside a stub, per body mode.  The
# 'trap' entry is per machine; 'direct' returns a constant and never traps.
def trap_off(mode, machine):
    return 0 if mode == 'direct' else MACHINES[machine]['trap_off']


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# spec file
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

def parse_spec(path):
    """Read a .thunks file -> (dll, exports, from_spec, excludes, extra).

    `extra` carries the COM-mode and foreign-surface directives:
        from_def       -- FROM-DEF <mod>.def: export list from a module
                          definition file (vkd3d-proton's d3d12.def).  A .def
                          carries neither arity nor types, so entries from it
                          rest on the clang oracle ALONE and are announced as
                          such -- there is no second reading to cross-check.
        com_json       -- COM-JSON <interfaces.json>: emit per-interface COM
                          stub arrays and __wine_com_thunk_info from the named
                          interface table (see the COM MODE banner below).
        probe_includes -- PROBE-INCLUDE <header>: the oracle's translation
                          unit becomes exactly these #includes, replacing the
                          Wine-centric default.  This is how a module whose
                          surface is NOT declared in Wine's headers (d3d12:
                          vkd3d-proton's own widl output) keeps the oracle
                          pointed at the headers its implementation was
                          actually compiled from.
        probe_extra    -- PROBE-EXTRA <header>: APPENDED to the oracle's
                          default translation unit, unlike PROBE-INCLUDE which
                          replaces it.  For a module whose surface is declared
                          in Wine's headers but ALSO in a private header of its
                          own (msvcrt's mtdll.h declares _lock/_unlock, which
                          no public header does).  Pair with INCLUDE-DIR.
        include_dirs   -- INCLUDE-DIR <dir>: extra -I directories for the
                          oracle, searched before Wine's include tree.
    Relative paths are relative to the .thunks file that names them.

    TWO ROLES, AND THE FILE SAYS WHICH.  Without a FROM-SPEC line the export
    lines ARE the list, exactly as they always were.  With one, the list comes
    from the module's own Wine .spec and the export lines become OVERRIDES for
    the handful of names that need something the .spec cannot say -- a
    `variadic=<v-variant>` opt-in, a .spec location for a name Wine declares in
    no header, or a deliberate arity/return assertion.  EXCLUDE drops a name.

    So a .thunks file becomes UNNECESSARY rather than forbidden: a module with
    nothing to override needs no file at all (--from-spec on the command line),
    one with a few needs only those lines, and the five hand-written ones keep
    working untouched.
    """
    dll = None
    from_spec = None
    excludes = []
    exports = []          # (name, imm32, nargs, returns_void, header, line)
    extra = dict(from_def=None, com_json=None, probe_includes=[],
                 probe_extra=[], include_dirs=[], guest_impl={}, guest_refuse={},
                 guest_pass={})
    with open(path) as f:
        for lineno, raw in enumerate(f, 1):
            line = raw.split('#', 1)[0].strip()
            if not line:
                continue
            parts = line.split()
            if parts[0].upper() == 'DLL':
                if len(parts) != 2:
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: DLL needs exactly one argument" % (path, lineno))
                dll = parts[1]
                continue
            if parts[0].upper() in ('FROM-DEF', 'COM-JSON'):
                key = parts[0].lower().replace('-', '_')
                if len(parts) != 2:
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: %s needs exactly one path"
                             % (path, lineno, parts[0]))
                if extra[key] is not None:
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: a second %s line" % (path, lineno, parts[0]))
                extra[key] = parts[1]
                continue
            # ---------------------------------------------------------------
            # Flat-surface interception directives (system-com-design.md
            # §4.3).  On a COM-JSON module, every flat export whose signature
            # carries interface pointers must be classified by exactly one of
            # these three, and the audit below FAILS THE BUILD otherwise:
            #
            #   GUEST-IMPL   <Export> <NativeName>  resolve the guest export
            #       to <NativeName> in the native namesake -- a wrapper that
            #       translates interface pointers at the classified positions.
            #       Rides the same impl_names_rva plumbing as variadic=;
            #       ntdll learns nothing.
            #   GUEST-REFUSE <Export> [reason]      resolve it to the shared native
            #       stub __wine_com_refuse, which answers E_NOTIMPL loudly --
            #       never a pass-through.  (The trapping export's name is in
            #       the dispatcher TRACE, so one shared stub suffices; the
            #       reason column documents WHY at the .thunks site.)
            #   GUEST-PASS   <Export> <reason>      an EXPLICIT audited
            #       pass-through: the export's interface-bearing type is
            #       provably inert on this path (VariantInit writes VT_EMPTY
            #       and reads nothing).  The reason is mandatory.
            # ---------------------------------------------------------------
            if parts[0].upper() == 'GUEST-IMPL':
                if len(parts) != 3:
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: GUEST-IMPL needs <Export> <NativeName>"
                             % (path, lineno))
                if parts[1] in extra['guest_impl']:
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: duplicate GUEST-IMPL for %s"
                             % (path, lineno, parts[1]))
                extra['guest_impl'][parts[1]] = parts[2]
                continue
            if parts[0].upper() in ('GUEST-REFUSE', 'GUEST-PASS'):
                key = 'guest_refuse' if parts[0].upper() == 'GUEST-REFUSE' \
                    else 'guest_pass'
                if len(parts) < 2 or (key == 'guest_pass' and len(parts) < 3):
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: %s needs <Export>%s"
                             % (path, lineno, parts[0].upper(),
                                ' <reason>' if key == 'guest_pass' else ''))
                if parts[1] in extra[key]:
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: duplicate %s for %s"
                             % (path, lineno, parts[0].upper(), parts[1]))
                extra[key][parts[1]] = ' '.join(parts[2:])
                continue
            if parts[0].upper() == 'PROBE-INCLUDE':
                if len(parts) != 2:
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: PROBE-INCLUDE needs exactly one header"
                             % (path, lineno))
                extra['probe_includes'].append(parts[1])
                continue
            if parts[0].upper() == 'PROBE-EXTRA':
                if len(parts) != 2:
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: PROBE-EXTRA needs exactly one header"
                             % (path, lineno))
                extra['probe_extra'].append(parts[1])
                continue
            if parts[0].upper() == 'INCLUDE-DIR':
                if len(parts) != 2:
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: INCLUDE-DIR needs exactly one directory"
                             % (path, lineno))
                extra['include_dirs'].append(parts[1])
                continue
            if parts[0].upper() == 'GUEST-MACHINE':
                # Read by tools/makedep.c, which decides WHICH machines to
                # invoke this tool for; the machine itself arrives here as
                # --machine.  Validated rather than ignored, so a typo is a
                # build error at the file that has it and not a silently
                # missing half.
                if len(parts) != 2 or parts[1] not in MACHINES:
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: GUEST-MACHINE needs one of: %s"
                             % (path, lineno, ', '.join(sorted(MACHINES))))
                continue
            if parts[0].upper() == 'FROM-SPEC':
                if len(parts) != 2:
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: FROM-SPEC needs exactly one argument: the "
                             "module's .spec, or 'auto' to look for "
                             "<name>.spec beside this file" % (path, lineno))
                if from_spec is not None:
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: a second FROM-SPEC line" % (path, lineno))
                from_spec = parts[1]
                continue
            if parts[0].upper() == 'EXCLUDE':
                if len(parts) != 2:
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: EXCLUDE needs exactly one export name"
                             % (path, lineno))
                excludes.append(parts[1])
                continue
            if len(parts) not in (5, 6):
                sys.exit("%s:%d: expected "
                         "'<Name> <imm32> <nargs> <int|void> <header.h>:<line>"
                         "[ variadic=<impl>]'" % (path, lineno))
            name, imm, nargs, retkind, loc = parts[:5]
            # Sixth token, optional: variadic=<name to resolve natively>.
            # Five tokens means exactly what it always meant.
            variadic_impl = None
            if len(parts) == 6:
                tok = parts[5]
                if not tok.startswith('variadic='):
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: unknown 6th token %r; the only one "
                             "defined is 'variadic=<impl>'" % (path, lineno, tok))
                variadic_impl = tok[len('variadic='):]
                if not variadic_impl:
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: variadic= needs the name of the "
                             "v-variant to call, e.g. variadic=vprintf"
                             % (path, lineno))
                if variadic_impl == name:
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: variadic=%s names the variadic export "
                             "itself; it must name that function's v-variant"
                             % (path, lineno, variadic_impl))
                if loc.endswith('.spec') or '.spec:' in loc:
                    sys.exit("%s:%d: variadic= requires a header location; a "
                             "Wine .spec carries no return type and its "
                             "`@ varargs` line lists only the fixed arguments, "
                             "so the v-variant cross-check is impossible"
                             % (path, lineno))
            try:
                imm = int(imm, 0)
                nargs = int(nargs, 0)
            except ValueError:
                sys.exit("%s:%d: bad number" % (path, lineno))
            if not (0 <= imm <= 0xffffffff):
                sys.exit("%s:%d: imm32 out of range" % (path, lineno))
            if retkind not in ('int', 'void'):
                sys.exit("%s:%d: return kind must be 'int' or 'void'" % (path, lineno))
            if ':' not in loc:
                sys.exit("%s:%d: location must be <header.h>:<line>" % (path, lineno))
            hdr, _, ln = loc.rpartition(':')
            try:
                ln = int(ln)
            except ValueError:
                sys.exit("%s:%d: bad line number in %r" % (path, lineno, loc))
            exports.append(dict(name=name, imm=imm, nargs=nargs,
                                returns_void=(retkind == 'void'),
                                header=hdr, line=ln,
                                variadic_impl=variadic_impl,
                                asserted=True,
                                specline=lineno))
    if dll is None:
        sys.exit("%s: no 'DLL <name>' line" % path)
    if not exports and from_spec is None and extra['from_def'] is None:
        sys.exit("%s: no exports" % path)
    if from_spec is not None and extra['from_def'] is not None:
        sys.exit("%s: FROM-SPEC and FROM-DEF are mutually exclusive -- one "
                 "module has one authoritative export list" % path)
    names = [e['name'] for e in exports]
    if len(set(names)) != len(names):
        sys.exit("%s: duplicate export names" % path)
    for reserved in RESERVED_EXPORTS:
        if reserved in names:
            sys.exit("%s: %s is reserved" % (path, reserved))
    return dll, exports, from_spec, excludes, extra


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE EXPORT LIST, DERIVED FROM THE MODULE'S OWN WINE .spec
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The .spec is the list nobody maintains twice: it is what winebuild turns into
# the module's real export table, so it is by construction the set of names a
# guest could import.  It supplies ONLY WHICH NAMES EXIST.  Every signature
# still comes from clang reading Wine's real headers -- a .spec has no return
# type at all and its `long` is 32 bits, so it cannot describe a thunk (see the
# banner at the top of wine_sig.py).
#
# What the .spec DOES state exactly is the arity and the CLASS of each
# argument, and that is used here as an independent CROSS-CHECK of what clang
# read, not as a source: an export whose .spec arity disagrees with its header
# declaration is refused and listed, never emitted on one source's word.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# tools/tools.h get_cpu_from_name(), which matches by PREFIX: the table entry
# has to be a prefix of the spelling in the file, so "x86_64" matches and a
# bare "x86" matches nothing.
_CPU_NAMES = (('i386', 'i386'), ('i486', 'i386'), ('i586', 'i386'),
              ('i686', 'i386'), ('i786', 'i386'), ('x86_64', 'x86_64'),
              ('amd64', 'x86_64'), ('aarch64', 'arm64'), ('arm64ec', 'arm64ec'),
              ('arm64', 'arm64'), ('arm', 'arm'), ('ppc64', 'powerpc64'),
              ('powerpc64', 'powerpc64'))
# tools/winebuild/build.h FLAG_CPU_WIN32 / FLAG_CPU_WIN64.
_CPU_WIN32 = frozenset(('i386', 'arm'))
_CPU_WIN64 = frozenset(('x86_64', 'arm64', 'arm64ec', 'powerpc64'))
_CPU_ALL = _CPU_WIN32 | _CPU_WIN64
# The machine the generated thunk PE is for.  Not configurable: the whole file
# is an AMD64 PE by construction (IMAGE_FILE_MACHINE_AMD64, MS-x64 stubs).
GUEST_CPU = 'x86_64'

# .spec entry types that are a callable function with an argument list.
_SPEC_FUNC_TYPES = frozenset(('stdcall', 'cdecl', 'varargs', 'thiscall',
                              'fastcall'))

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ORDINALS -- WHICH NUMBER EACH EXPORT ANSWERS TO
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A guest may import BY ORDINAL and carry no name at all.  Steam's own
# d3ddriverquery64.exe does exactly that: its d3d12.dll import descriptor names
# ordinal 101 and nothing else, because that is the ordinal Microsoft's
# d3d12.dll has always exported D3D12CreateDevice at, and Wine's d3d12.spec and
# vkd3d-proton's d3d12.def both pin it there for that reason.
#
# So the thunk PE has to export that function AT THAT ORDINAL.  It did not: the
# .def this tool wrote listed names only, and lld-link then numbers a plain
# EXPORTS list 1..N in NAME order, which is not what any of these modules
# publishes.  The guest's loader looked up 101 in a table numbered 1..8, found
# nothing, and bound ntdll's missing-import sentinel -- so the first call went
# to 0xdead0001 from d3ddriverquery64.exe+0x108e with no symbol anywhere near
# it.  Ordinals are not the linker's business here; every export goes into the
# .def with the number its own source pins.
#
# assign_ordinals() computes those numbers exactly the way the real module's
# do get computed, by tools/winebuild/parser.c:assign_ordinals():
#
#   * base  = the LOWEST EXPLICIT ordinal in the file, or 1 if there is none
#     (NOT 1 unconditionally -- d3d12 pins 101 and 102 and nothing else, so its
#     base is 101 and its unnumbered exports start at 103);
#   * every entry with no ordinal of its own takes the lowest free number from
#     base upwards, IN FILE ORDER;
#   * an entry this machine does not export at all (-arch=/-i386) is filtered
#     out BEFORE any of that and consumes no number, exactly where winebuild's
#     assign_exports() filters it.
#
# The entries this tool cannot thunk -- stubs, -noname, -register, and every
# export the signature oracle refuses -- DO take part in the numbering and are
# then simply not emitted.  Their ordinals become HOLES in the thunk's export
# table, which is the honest outcome: a guest importing one of them reads a
# zero address-table entry, ntdll names it in a WARN and binds a sentinel, and
# every surviving export keeps the number the real module gives it.  Closing
# the holes by renumbering would silently bind a guest's import of a refused
# export to an unrelated function.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

MAX_ORDINALS = 65535            # tools/winebuild/build.h


def assign_ordinals(records, path):
    """Number a module's entry points the way winebuild does.

    `records` are the entry points that survive the machine filter, in file
    order; each is a dict with 'ordinal' (an int if the file pinned one, else
    None) and 'lineno'.  Each record comes back with 'ordinal' filled in and
    'ordinal_pinned' saying whether the file was the one that chose it.
    Returns the highest ordinal in use, which is the module's whole ordinal
    space: nothing this tool adds of its own may land inside it.
    """
    used = {}
    for r in records:
        r['ordinal_pinned'] = r['ordinal'] is not None
        if r['ordinal'] is None:
            continue
        if not 0 < r['ordinal'] < MAX_ORDINALS:
            sys.exit('%s:%d: ordinal %d is out of range 1..%d'
                     % (path, r['lineno'], r['ordinal'], MAX_ORDINALS - 1))
        if r['ordinal'] in used:
            sys.exit('%s:%d: ordinal %d redefined; first defined at line %d'
                     % (path, r['lineno'], r['ordinal'], used[r['ordinal']]))
        used[r['ordinal']] = r['lineno']
    nxt = min(used) if used else 1
    for r in records:
        if r['ordinal'] is not None:
            continue
        while nxt in used:
            nxt += 1
        if nxt >= MAX_ORDINALS:
            sys.exit('%s:%d: too many entry points (max %d)'
                     % (path, r['lineno'], MAX_ORDINALS))
        r['ordinal'] = nxt
        used[nxt] = r['lineno']
    return max(used) if used else 0


def _cpu_from_name(tok):
    for prefix, cpu in _CPU_NAMES:
        if tok.startswith(prefix):
            return cpu
    return None


def _spec_cpus(flags):
    """-> set of CPUs an entry is exported for, or None for "all of them".

    Reimplements tools/winebuild/parser.c parse_spec_flags()'s -arch= handling
    and its `-i386` backwards-compatibility alias, including the `!cpu`
    negation and the "x86-64 implies arm64ec" rule.
    """
    cpus, mask = set(), set()
    for f in flags:
        if f == 'i386':                        # backwards compatibility
            cpus.add('i386')
        elif f.startswith('arch='):
            for tok in f[len('arch='):].split(','):
                if tok == 'win32':
                    cpus |= _CPU_WIN32
                elif tok == 'win64':
                    cpus |= _CPU_WIN64
                else:
                    neg = tok.startswith('!')
                    cpu = _cpu_from_name(tok[1:] if neg else tok)
                    if cpu is None:
                        raise SystemExit("unknown architecture %r in -%s"
                                         % (tok, f))
                    (mask if neg else cpus).add(cpu)
    if 'x86_64' in cpus:
        cpus.add('arm64ec')
    if 'x86_64' in mask:
        mask.add('arm64ec')
    if mask:
        cpus |= _CPU_ALL - mask
    return cpus or None


def parse_wine_spec(path, mangled_ok=()):
    """Read a Wine .spec -> (ordered entries, skipped-with-reason, ord limit).

    An entry is dict(name, ordinal, spec_args, spec_type, flags, specline).
    `spec_args` is the argument-type list exactly as the .spec spells it, kept
    for the arity/class cross-check; it is never the source of a signature.
    `ordinal` is the number the real module answers to, from assign_ordinals().

    Everything the guest cannot bind to a named AMD64 export is skipped here
    rather than refused later, with the reason, so the two are not confused in
    the report: a `@ stub` has no implementation to call at all, and an
    -arch-restricted entry is simply not in this machine's export table.

    The machine filter runs FIRST, before anything else about the line is
    looked at, because that is where winebuild's assign_exports() runs it and
    an entry that fails it consumes no ordinal.  Everything that passes it is
    an entry point of the real module and is numbered, whether this tool can
    thunk it or not.
    """
    entries, skipped, records = [], [], []
    with open(path, errors='replace') as f:
        for lineno, raw in enumerate(f, 1):
            line = raw.split('#', 1)[0].strip()
            if not line:
                continue
            parts = line.split()
            if len(parts) < 2:
                continue
            ordinal, kind, rest = parts[0], parts[1], parts[2:]
            if ordinal != '@' and not ordinal.isdigit():
                continue                       # not an export line at all
            flags = []
            while rest and rest[0].startswith('-'):
                flags.append(rest.pop(0)[1:])
            cpus = _spec_cpus(flags)
            if cpus is not None and GUEST_CPU not in cpus:
                skipped.append((rest[0].split('(')[0] if rest else '?', lineno,
                                'not exported for %s (-%s)'
                                % (GUEST_CPU,
                                   ' -'.join(f for f in flags
                                             if f == 'i386' or f.startswith('arch=')))))
                continue
            if not rest:
                continue
            rec = dict(lineno=lineno,
                       ordinal=int(ordinal) if ordinal != '@' else None)
            records.append(rec)
            if kind == 'stub':
                skipped.append((rest[0].split('(')[0], lineno, 'stub -- Wine '
                                'has no implementation to call'))
                continue
            if kind not in _SPEC_FUNC_TYPES:
                # extern/variable/equate: data, not a callable entry point.
                skipped.append((rest[0].split('(')[0], lineno,
                                '`@ %s` is not a callable entry point' % kind))
                continue
            decl = ' '.join(rest)
            name, _, tail = decl.partition('(')
            name = name.strip()
            args = tail.rpartition(')')[0].split() if '(' in decl else []
            # The token after the closing paren, when there is one, names the
            # implementation the export is an ALIAS of -- ucrtbase's whole
            # `_o_*` surface is `@ cdecl _o__close(long) _close` and its kind.
            # Kept only when it is a same-module plain C identifier: a dotted
            # target is a forward into another module, and a mangled one is
            # nothing the header oracle could declare.  The header oracle can
            # then resolve the TARGET's declaration when the alias's own name
            # is declared nowhere, which for `_o__close` it never is.
            impl = tail.rpartition(')')[2].strip() if '(' in decl else ''
            alias = (impl if impl and impl != name and '.' not in impl
                     and impl.replace('_', 'a').isalnum() else None)
            if name == '@':
                # `86 stdcall -i386 -private @(ptr) krnl386...`: exported by
                # ordinal under no name at all.
                skipped.append((name, lineno, '-noname: exported by ordinal '
                                'only, so there is no name to bind'))
                continue
            if not name.replace('_', 'a').replace('.', 'a').isalnum() \
                    and name not in mangled_ok:
                # The filter is about the ORACLE, not about the export table:
                # the header oracle resolves a name by compiling a translation
                # unit that declares it, and `??2@YAPEAX_K@Z` is not something
                # C can declare.  A PE export table holds arbitrary strings and
                # never cared.  So a name that cites a .spec LOCATION is let
                # through by the caller (see exports_from_wine_spec): that path
                # reads the arity straight off the .spec line and compiles
                # nothing, which is exactly the case the filter was protecting.
                skipped.append((name, lineno, 'name is not a plain C identifier'))
                continue
            if 'noname' in flags:
                # winebuild puts no name in the export table for these; a guest
                # can only reach them by ordinal, which a thunk PE built from
                # names cannot serve.
                skipped.append((name, lineno, '-noname: exported by ordinal '
                                'only, so there is no name to bind'))
                continue
            if 'register' in flags:
                skipped.append((name, lineno, '-register: the entry point takes '
                                'a CONTEXT, not an argument list'))
                continue
            entries.append(dict(name=name, spec_args=args, spec_type=kind,
                                flags=flags, specline=lineno, spec_alias=alias,
                                rec=rec))
    ord_limit = assign_ordinals(records, path)
    for e in entries:
        rec = e.pop('rec')
        e['ordinal'], e['ordinal_pinned'] = rec['ordinal'], rec['ordinal_pinned']
    if not entries:
        sys.exit('%s: no usable export lines' % path)
    return entries, skipped, ord_limit


def exports_from_wine_spec(path, overrides=None, excludes=(), start_imm=0):
    """-> (export entries in .spec order, skipped, dropped-by-EXCLUDE, limit).

    `overrides` maps an export name to a parsed .thunks line, which then
    supplies the assertion columns (and `variadic=`) for that one export
    exactly as it does in --spec mode.  Anything not overridden carries
    asserted=False and is checked against the .spec's own arity instead.
    """
    overrides = overrides or {}
    # An override that cites a .spec LOCATION needs no header and compiles no
    # C, so the "plain C identifier" filter in parse_wine_spec has nothing to
    # protect for it.  Lifting it exactly there is what lets a MANGLED C++
    # export be served: `??2@YAPEAX_K@Z` is `void *operator new(size_t)` --
    # one integer-class argument, a pointer back, no `this` and no EH state --
    # and dlls/msvcr100/msvcr100.spec declares it with its arity and its
    # implementation.  Only names with an explicit hand-written row get this;
    # everything else still has to be something the oracle can declare.
    mangled_ok = {n for n, ov in overrides.items()
                  if (ov.get('header') or '').endswith('.spec')}
    entries, skipped, ord_limit = parse_wine_spec(path, mangled_ok=mangled_ok)
    excluded = set(excludes)
    out, dropped = [], []
    for e in entries:
        if e['name'] in excluded:
            dropped.append(e['name'])
            continue
        ov = overrides.get(e['name'])
        if ov:
            e = dict(ov, spec_args=e['spec_args'], spec_type=e['spec_type'],
                     specline=e['specline'], ordinal=e['ordinal'],
                     ordinal_pinned=e['ordinal_pinned'], asserted=True)
        else:
            # No hand-written assertion to check: the header oracle is the only
            # source, cross-checked against this line's own arity.  imm is only
            # read by --body=direct, where it just has to be distinct per stub.
            e = dict(e, imm=start_imm + len(out), nargs=None,
                     returns_void=None, header=None, line=None,
                     variadic_impl=None, asserted=False)
        e['imm'] = e.get('imm', start_imm + len(out))
        out.append(e)
    unknown = sorted(set(overrides) - {e['name'] for e in entries})
    if unknown:
        skipped_names = {n: why for n, _l, why in skipped}
        sys.exit('%s: %d override(s) name an export this .spec does not offer:\n  '
                 % (path, len(unknown))
                 + '\n  '.join('%-30s %s' % (n, skipped_names.get(
                     n, 'no such line in the .spec at all'))
                     for n in unknown))
    return out, skipped, dropped, ord_limit


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE EXPORT LIST, DERIVED FROM A MODULE DEFINITION (.def) FILE
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# For a graphics module the export surface we promise the guest is the surface
# the NATIVE TARGET implements -- vkd3d-proton's d3d12.def, not Wine's
# d3d12.spec.  Generating a graphics thunk from a Wine .spec toward Wine's own
# implementation is the one construction this tool must keep unrepresentable
# (the native namesake would be Wine's forwarder into libs/vkd3d, exactly the
# implementation the port replaces), so COM-mode modules name a .def instead.
#
# A .def carries ONLY names: no types, no arity, not even an argument list.
# Every signature therefore rests on the clang oracle alone, with no second
# reading to cross-check -- weaker than the .spec path, and announced loudly
# at generation time rather than papered over.  The oracle still refuses
# anything it cannot prove is register-passable, exactly as everywhere else.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

def parse_module_def(path):
    """Read a .def -> (library name or None, entry points, ordinal limit).

    An entry point is dict(name, ordinal, ordinal_pinned); `name` is None for
    the ones a thunk cannot serve.  Those are RETURNED rather than dropped
    because a NONAME or DATA entry still occupies its ordinal in the module the
    guest expects, and the numbering below has to count it -- see the ORDINALS
    banner.  Nothing else looks at them.
    """
    library, entries, in_exports = None, [], False
    with open(path, errors='replace') as f:
        for lineno, raw in enumerate(f, 1):
            line = raw.split(';', 1)[0].strip()
            if not line:
                continue
            parts = line.split()
            key = parts[0].upper()
            if key == 'LIBRARY':
                library = parts[1] if len(parts) > 1 else None
                in_exports = False
                continue
            if key == 'EXPORTS':
                in_exports = True
                parts = parts[1:]
                if not parts:
                    continue
            elif not in_exports:
                continue
            name = parts[0]
            ordinal = None
            for p in parts[1:]:
                if p.startswith('@') and p[1:].isdigit():
                    ordinal = int(p[1:])
            e = dict(name=name, ordinal=ordinal, lineno=lineno)
            entries.append(e)
            # `Name @ordinal` and `Name @ordinal NONAME` forms: the name still
            # exists in the export table unless NONAME says otherwise, and a
            # thunk binds by name, so NONAME entries are skipped like -noname.
            if any(p.upper() in ('NONAME', 'DATA') for p in parts[1:]):
                e['name'] = None
                continue
            if '=' in name:
                name = name.split('=', 1)[0]
            if not name.replace('_', 'a').isalnum():
                sys.exit('%s:%d: export name %r is not a plain C identifier'
                         % (path, lineno, name))
            e['name'] = name
    names = [e['name'] for e in entries if e['name']]
    if not names:
        sys.exit('%s: no export names' % path)
    if len(set(names)) != len(names):
        sys.exit('%s: duplicate export names' % path)
    ord_limit = assign_ordinals(entries, path)
    return library, entries, ord_limit


def exports_from_module_def(path, overrides=None, excludes=(), start_imm=0):
    """-> (library, export entries in .def order, dropped-by-EXCLUDE, limit)."""
    overrides = overrides or {}
    library, entries, ord_limit = parse_module_def(path)
    entries = [e for e in entries if e['name']]
    excluded = set(excludes)
    out, dropped = [], []
    for entry in entries:
        name = entry['name']
        if name in excluded:
            dropped.append(name)
            continue
        ov = overrides.get(name)
        if ov:
            e = dict(ov, asserted=True)
        else:
            e = dict(name=name, imm=start_imm + len(out), nargs=None,
                     returns_void=None, header=None, line=None,
                     variadic_impl=None, asserted=False, spec_args=None,
                     specline=0)
        e['imm'] = e.get('imm', start_imm + len(out))
        e['ordinal'] = entry['ordinal']
        e['ordinal_pinned'] = entry['ordinal_pinned']
        out.append(e)
    unknown = sorted(set(overrides) - {e['name'] for e in entries})
    if unknown:
        sys.exit('%s: %d override(s) name an export this .def does not offer: %s'
                 % (path, len(unknown), ', '.join(unknown)))
    return library, out, dropped, ord_limit


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COM MODE: per-interface stub arrays + __wine_com_thunk_info
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Input is an interfaces JSON (ppc64le/dxvk/gen_interfaces.py output, and its
# ppc64le/audio and ppc64le/syscom siblings, derived from the
# implementation's own widl-generated headers): per interface
# a uuid and the FULL flattened vtable slot list.  The module gains, alongside
# the flat-export machinery which is completely unchanged:
#
#   * one contiguous stub array per interface type -- slot_count stubs of the
#     SAME 5-byte trap body at the same 16-byte stride as the flat stubs;
#   * an exported __wine_com_thunk_info:
#
#         struct com_thunk_info
#         {
#             UINT version;      /* COM_INFO_VERSION */
#             UINT iface_count;
#             UINT stride;       /* 16 */
#             UINT trap_off;     /* 3: mov r10,rcx ; syscall */
#             UINT ifaces_rva;   /* iface_count x { GUID iid;
#                                   UINT slot_count; UINT stubs_rva; } */
#         };
#
# The guest-facing vtable for interface i is simply the address sequence
# stubs_rva + n*stride, materialised by the native module's runtime at attach;
# a trapping RIP maps back to (iface, slot) by the same exact-hit arithmetic
# the flat stubs use.  NO marshalling knowledge lives here: the module is pure
# trap surface, which is what lets one generator serve every COM module (d3d12
# now, dxgi/d3d11 later) unchanged.
#
# INTERFACE ORDER IS SORTED BY NAME.  The dispatcher hands the native module a
# bare (iface, slot) pair, so the guest module's interface indices and the
# native module's generated tables must agree; both derive from the same JSON
# and both sort by name, and spec2thunk-check re-derives the order from the
# JSON independently.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

COM_INFO_VERSION = 1


def parse_guid(text):
    """'aaaaaaaa-bbbb-cccc-dddd-eeeeeeeeeeee' -> 16 GUID bytes as Windows
    lays them out: Data1/2/3 little-endian, Data4 as written."""
    m = re.fullmatch(r'([0-9a-fA-F]{8})-([0-9a-fA-F]{4})-([0-9a-fA-F]{4})-'
                     r'([0-9a-fA-F]{4})-([0-9a-fA-F]{12})', text.strip())
    if not m:
        raise ValueError('malformed uuid %r' % text)
    d1, d2, d3 = (int(m.group(i), 16) for i in (1, 2, 3))
    d4 = bytes.fromhex(m.group(4) + m.group(5))
    return struct.pack('<IHH', d1, d2, d3) + d4


def load_com_interfaces(path):
    """-> [dict(name, uuid(bytes), slot_count)], sorted by interface name."""
    import json
    with open(path) as f:
        d = json.load(f)
    ifaces = d['interfaces']
    out = []
    for name in sorted(ifaces):
        i = ifaces[name]
        slots = i['slots']
        # The JSON's slot list is the FULL flattened vtable in order; refuse a
        # table with holes rather than emit a stub array whose index does not
        # mean the slot number.
        want = list(range(len(slots)))
        got = [s['slot'] for s in slots]
        if got != want:
            sys.exit('%s: %s slot numbers are not 0..%d contiguous'
                     % (path, name, len(slots) - 1))
        if not slots:
            sys.exit('%s: %s has no slots' % (path, name))
        try:
            uuid = parse_guid(i['uuid'])
        except ValueError as e:
            sys.exit('%s: %s: %s' % (path, name, e))
        out.append(dict(name=name, uuid=uuid, slot_count=len(slots)))
    dup = len(out) - len({i['uuid'] for i in out})
    if dup:
        sys.exit('%s: %d duplicate interface IIDs' % (path, dup))
    return out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FLAT-SURFACE AUDIT (system-com-design.md §4.3), for FROM-SPEC modules
# with COM-JSON: no interface-bearing flat export may pass through silently.
#
# A flat pass-through writes whatever the native export wrote -- including a
# native interface pointer -- straight into guest-visible memory, and the
# guest's first vtable call through it executes ppc64 bytes as x86-64.  So
# every emitted export whose clang-verified signature carries an
# interface-pointer-bearing type (directly, via a pointer typedef the roster
# JSON names, via a known carrier struct, or as a void**/LPVOID* out slot)
# must be classified by exactly one GUEST-IMPL / GUEST-REFUSE / GUEST-PASS
# directive, and the build FAILS on an unclassified one.  Stale directives
# (naming an unemitted export, or one whose signature is not bearing) fail
# too: a dead entry is indistinguishable from a live one.
#
# The wrapper/refusal symbols must exist: each GUEST-IMPL target and each
# __wine_guest_refuse_<name> is required to appear in the native namesake's
# .spec, so a missing wrapper is a build failure here rather than a guest
# fault at first call.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

_COM_CARRIER_STRUCTS = frozenset((
    'MULTI_QI', 'STGMEDIUM', 'uSTGMEDIUM', 'VARIANT', 'VARIANTARG',
    'PROPVARIANT', 'SAFEARRAY', 'LPVARIANT', 'LPPROPVARIANT', 'LPSAFEARRAY'))

# An out slot typed as the UNIVERSAL interface rather than a rostered one.
# `IUnknown **ppApo` is every bit as interface-bearing as `IXAudio2 **`, and it
# used to be invisible here: the roster names concrete interfaces, IUnknown is
# in no roster, and the void**/LPVOID* rule below does not match a named type.
# So an export vending an object through IUnknown** read as "not bearing", a
# GUEST-REFUSE on it was reported STALE, and the only way to stop it passing a
# native vtable through to a guest was to EXCLUDE it -- which is exactly what
# dlls/xaudio2_9/xaudio2_9.thunks had to do to CreateFX, CreateAudioReverb and
# CreateAudioVolumeMeter, and it recorded the blind spot rather than working
# around it quietly.  This is that blind spot closed.
#
# DOUBLE POINTER ONLY, matching the shape of every other rule here.  What this
# audit is about is stated at the top of the block: a pass-through "writes
# whatever the native export wrote -- including a native interface pointer --
# straight into guest-visible memory", which is an OUT parameter.  A singular
# `IUnknown *pUnkOuter` is an IN parameter -- COM aggregation -- and the hazard
# there is the opposite one (a guest object reaching native code, which wants a
# reverse proxy, not a wrap).  Folding that in here would be a different claim
# under the same name, so it is deliberately not folded in.
# LPUNKNOWN takes ONE star, not two: it is already `IUnknown *`, so
# `LPUNKNOWN *ppUnk` is the double pointer and `LPUNKNOWN pUnkOuter` is the
# singular IN parameter this rule must not claim.  That is the same asymmetry
# the LPVOID rule below has always had, for the same reason -- and writing
# both spellings with two stars would have matched a triple pointer nothing
# declares while missing the OUT slot that does exist.
_COM_UNIVERSAL_IFACE_RE = re.compile(
    r'\bIUnknown\s*\*\s*\*|\bLPUNKNOWN\s*\*')


def com_flat_bearing(sig, iface_tokens):
    """Which of this signature's positions carry interface pointers."""
    hits = []
    for i, p in enumerate(sig.get('params') or []):
        toks = set(re.findall(r'\w+', p or ''))
        if toks & iface_tokens:
            hits.append('%d:%s' % (i, p))
        elif toks & _COM_CARRIER_STRUCTS:
            hits.append('%d:%s' % (i, p))
        elif re.search(r'void\s*\*\s*\*', p or '') or \
                re.search(r'\bLPVOID\s*\*', p or ''):
            hits.append('%d:%s' % (i, p))
        elif _COM_UNIVERSAL_IFACE_RE.search(p or ''):
            hits.append('%d:%s' % (i, p))
    r = sig.get('ret') or ''
    if set(re.findall(r'\w+', r)) & (iface_tokens | _COM_CARRIER_STRUCTS):
        hits.append('ret:%s' % r)
    return hits


def apply_guest_impl(exports, extra, native_spec_path):
    """GUEST-IMPL on a module with no COM-JSON.

    The COM audit's job is completeness -- proving no interface-bearing export
    slipped through unclassified -- and that question does not exist here.  The
    checks that DO still apply are the ones that catch a directive which cannot
    work, and they are the same three:

      * the named export must be one this module actually emits (an
        oracle-refused export is already absent and binds to a sentinel, so a
        redirect on it would silently do nothing);
      * the native symbol must be exported by the native namesake's .spec, or
        the guest faults at first call instead of reaching the wrapper;
      * it cannot be combined with variadic=, which needs impl_names_rva for
        its own v-variant forwarding.
    """
    by_name = {e['name']: e for e in exports}
    errs = []

    for n, impl in sorted(extra['guest_impl'].items()):
        e = by_name.get(n)
        if e is None:
            errs.append('GUEST-IMPL names %s, which this module does not emit '
                        '(oracle-refused exports are already absent and bind '
                        'to named sentinels)' % n)
            continue
        if e['sig'].get('variadic'):
            errs.append('GUEST-IMPL %s: cannot combine with variadic=' % n)

    with open(native_spec_path) as f:
        spec_text = f.read()
    for sym in sorted(set(extra['guest_impl'].values())):
        if not re.search(r'\b%s\b' % re.escape(sym), spec_text):
            errs.append('%s is not exported by %s; the guest would fault at '
                        'first call instead of reaching the wrapper'
                        % (sym, os.path.basename(native_spec_path)))

    if errs:
        sys.exit('FATAL: GUEST-IMPL is not sound:\n  ' + '\n  '.join(errs))

    for e in exports:
        if e['name'] in extra['guest_impl']:
            e['impl'] = extra['guest_impl'][e['name']]

    print('GUEST-IMPL  %d export(s) redirected: %s'
          % (len(extra['guest_impl']),
             ', '.join('%s -> %s' % kv
                       for kv in sorted(extra['guest_impl'].items()))))
    print()


def audit_guest_com_flat(exports, com_json_path, extra, native_spec_path):
    """Classify-or-die, then apply the GUEST-IMPL/GUEST-REFUSE redirects.
    Mutates exports' impl names in place."""
    import json
    with open(com_json_path) as f:
        d = json.load(f)
    iface_tokens = set(d['interfaces']) | set(d.get('iface_ptr_aliases', {}))

    classified = {}
    for tab, kind in ((extra['guest_impl'], 'GUEST-IMPL'),
                      (extra['guest_refuse'], 'GUEST-REFUSE'),
                      (extra['guest_pass'], 'GUEST-PASS')):
        for n in tab:
            if n in classified:
                sys.exit('AUDIT: %s is classified twice (%s and %s)'
                         % (n, classified[n], kind))
            classified[n] = kind

    by_name = {e['name']: e for e in exports}
    errs, unclassified = [], []
    for n, kind in sorted(classified.items()):
        if n not in by_name:
            errs.append('%s names %s, which this module does not emit '
                        '(oracle-refused exports are already absent and '
                        'bind to named sentinels)' % (kind, n))
    for e in exports:
        hits = com_flat_bearing(e['sig'], iface_tokens)
        kind = classified.get(e['name'])
        if hits and not kind:
            unclassified.append((e['name'], hits))
        elif kind and not hits:
            errs.append('%s %s: clang sees no interface-bearing type in its '
                        'signature; stale directive' % (kind, e['name']))
        if kind == 'GUEST-IMPL' and e['sig'].get('variadic'):
            errs.append('GUEST-IMPL %s: cannot combine with variadic='
                        % e['name'])

    if unclassified:
        lines = ['  %-36s %s' % (n, '; '.join(h)) for n, h in unclassified]
        errs.append('%d interface-bearing flat export(s) with no '
                    'GUEST-IMPL/GUEST-REFUSE/GUEST-PASS classification:\n%s'
                    % (len(unclassified), '\n'.join(lines)))

    # the wrapper symbols must exist in the native namesake's .spec
    with open(native_spec_path) as f:
        spec_text = f.read()
    need = sorted(set(extra['guest_impl'].values())
                  | ({'__wine_com_refuse'} if extra['guest_refuse'] else set()))
    for sym in need:
        if not re.search(r'\b%s\b' % re.escape(sym), spec_text):
            errs.append('%s is not exported by %s; the guest would fault at '
                        'first call instead of reaching the wrapper'
                        % (sym, os.path.basename(native_spec_path)))

    if errs:
        sys.exit('FATAL: the COM flat-surface audit failed:\n  '
                 + '\n  '.join(errs))

    for e in exports:
        if e['name'] in extra['guest_impl']:
            e['impl'] = extra['guest_impl'][e['name']]
        elif e['name'] in extra['guest_refuse']:
            e['impl'] = '__wine_com_refuse'

    n_bearing = sum(1 for e in exports
                    if com_flat_bearing(e['sig'], iface_tokens))
    print('COM FLAT AUDIT  %d interface-bearing export(s): %d wrapped, %d '
          'refused, %d audited pass-throughs; %d clean pass-throughs'
          % (n_bearing, len(extra['guest_impl']), len(extra['guest_refuse']),
             len(extra['guest_pass']), len(exports) - n_bearing))
    print()


def emit_com_asm(a, com_ifaces, machine='x86_64'):
    """Append the COM stub arrays (to .thunk) and info tables (to .rdata).
    Called with the flat sections already emitted; `a` is the line sink."""
    a('\t.section\t.thunk,"xr"')
    a('\t.balign\t%d' % STRIDE)
    body = stub_bytes('trap', 0, machine=machine)
    for i, itf in enumerate(com_ifaces):
        a('\t.globl\twine_com_stubs_%d' % i)
        a('wine_com_stubs_%d:\t\t# %s, %d slots'
          % (i, itf['name'], itf['slot_count']))
        for _ in range(itf['slot_count']):
            a('\t.byte\t' + ','.join('0x%02X' % c for c in body))
    a('')
    a('\t.section\t.rdata,"dr"')
    a('\t.balign\t8')
    a('\t.globl\twine_com_thunk_info')
    a('wine_com_thunk_info:')
    a('\t.long\t%d\t\t\t# version' % COM_INFO_VERSION)
    a('\t.long\t%d\t\t\t# iface_count' % len(com_ifaces))
    a('\t.long\t%d\t\t\t# stride' % STRIDE)
    a('\t.long\t%d\t\t\t# trap_off' % trap_off('trap', machine))
    a('\t.long\twine_com_ifacev@IMGREL\t# ifaces_rva')
    a('')
    a('\t.balign\t4')
    a('wine_com_ifacev:')
    for i, itf in enumerate(com_ifaces):
        a('\t# %s' % itf['name'])
        a('\t.byte\t' + ','.join('0x%02X' % c for c in itf['uuid']))
        a('\t.long\t%d' % itf['slot_count'])
        a('\t.long\twine_com_stubs_%d@IMGREL' % i)
    a('')


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# signature resolution against Wine's headers
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

def resolve_signatures(exports, headers, specs=None):
    """-> (accepted, refused).  Hard-fails on a spec/header disagreement.

    The location column normally names a Wine HEADER, and that entry is fully
    verified by clang.  If it names a Wine `.spec` file instead, the entry
    takes the last-resort arity oracle (wine_sig.WineSpecs) -- read the class
    docstring, in particular that its <int|void> column is an UNCHECKED human
    assertion.  Those entries are collected and reported separately.

    AN ENTRY WITH asserted=False CAME FROM A WINE .spec, NOT FROM A HAND-WRITTEN
    LINE, so there is no nargs / int|void / header:line claim to police.  The
    .spec's OWN argument list is used as the cross-check instead: a disagreement
    between it and the header declaration is a REFUSAL of that one export, not a
    fatal error for the module.  A hand-written line is a promise somebody made
    and a broken one is a bug; a whole-.spec run legitimately meets exports Wine
    describes inconsistently, and killing the build over each is useless.
    """
    # ONE clang run for the whole module's stage-2 probes instead of one per
    # export.  This decides nothing on its own -- see WineHeaders.prefetch():
    # a clean batch memoises acceptances, anything a diagnostic touches still
    # takes its own single-symbol probe and its own message.  Ask for the
    # v-variants too, since every variadic makes a second signature() call.
    headers.prefetch(
        [(e['name'], e.get('variadic_impl') is not None)
         for e in exports if not (e['header'] or '').endswith('.spec')]
        + [(e['variadic_impl'], False)
           for e in exports if e.get('variadic_impl')]
        + [(e['spec_alias'], False)
           for e in exports if e.get('spec_alias')
           and not (e['header'] or '').endswith('.spec')])

    accepted, refused, mismatches = [], [], []
    for e in exports:
        # Register-contract exports carry their own guest body and never meet
        # the oracle: there is no C declaration to find and no call to
        # marshal.  See RAW_BODY_EXPORTS.
        if e['name'] in RAW_BODY_EXPORTS:
            e = dict(e)
            e['sig'] = dict(nargs=0, returns_void=True, variadic=False,
                            header=e.get('header'), line=e.get('line'),
                            qualtype='raw guest body')
            e['desc'] = wine_sig.descriptor(e['sig'])
            e['impl'] = e['name']
            e['raw_body'] = RAW_BODY_EXPORTS[e['name']]
            accepted.append(e)
            continue
        want_variadic = e.get('variadic_impl') is not None
        # A .spec states the CLASS of each argument exactly, and that is one
        # thing it is authoritative about.  Refuse on it before spending a
        # header lookup, so the reason names the .spec that said so.
        #
        # Only int128 is refused here now.  `double` and `float` used to be,
        # because a floating-point argument had nowhere to go; they are
        # carried now (marshal_thunk_args_fp/call_native_thunk_fp in ntdll),
        # so they have to reach the header oracle -- which is the only thing
        # that can tell a double from a float, say whether the RETURN is
        # floating point, and refuse an FP argument past XMM3.  The .spec
        # cannot answer any of those, so refusing on it here would decide the
        # question with the one source that does not know the answer.
        bad = [a for a in (e.get('spec_args') or [])
               if a in wine_sig.WineSpecs.PREFILTER_BAD_ARGS]
        if bad and not e.get('asserted', True):
            refused.append((e['name'], '%s (%s:%d)'
                            % (wine_sig.WineSpecs.PREFILTER_BAD_ARGS[bad[0]],
                               os.path.basename(e.get('spec_path', '.spec')),
                               e['specline'])))
            continue
        bad = []
        if False:
            refused.append((e['name'], '%s (%s:%d)'
                            % (wine_sig.WineSpecs.BAD_ARGS[bad[0]],
                               os.path.basename(e.get('spec_path', '.spec')),
                               e['specline'])))
            continue
        try:
            if (e['header'] or '').endswith('.spec'):
                if specs is None:
                    raise Refused('.spec location cited but no source tree given')
                # The cited line is provably the applicable one when it is
                # the very line this tool's own -arch= filter kept for this
                # name (specline), which is winebuild's rule run over the whole
                # .spec.  Only then may the oracle accept an -arch= line; a
                # human citing the arm form of a name that also has a win64
                # form still gets refused.
                sig = specs.signature(e['name'], e['header'], e['line'],
                                      e['returns_void'],
                                      arch_verified=(e.get('line') is not None
                                                     and e.get('line')
                                                         == e.get('specline')))
            else:
                try:
                    sig = headers.signature(e['name'],
                                            allow_variadic=want_variadic)
                except Refused as ex:
                    # An export that is a same-module ALIAS in its .spec
                    # (`@ cdecl _o__close(long) _close`) has the TARGET's
                    # signature by construction -- winebuild binds both names
                    # to the one implementation -- so when the alias's own
                    # name is declared in no header, the target's declaration
                    # answers for it.  Only the no-declaration refusal may be
                    # rescued this way: an alias whose own name IS declared is
                    # governed by that declaration, and any other refusal
                    # (struct class, varargs) is about the shape, which the
                    # alias shares.  The native module exports the alias name
                    # itself, so the host still resolves e['name'] unchanged.
                    if not (e.get('spec_alias')
                            and ex.reason.startswith('no declaration found')):
                        raise
                    sig = headers.signature(e['spec_alias'],
                                            allow_variadic=want_variadic)
        except Refused as ex:
            refused.append((e['name'], ex.reason))
            continue
        # ------------------------------------------------------------------
        # variadic= is an ASSERTION like every other column, and it is checked
        # in BOTH directions.  A variadic with no variadic= never gets here at
        # all -- allow_variadic stayed False and wine_sig refused it -- so the
        # only direction left to police is a variadic= on something that is not
        # in fact variadic, which would make the host synthesise a va_list out
        # of whatever happened to follow the last real argument.
        # ------------------------------------------------------------------
        if want_variadic and not sig.get('variadic'):
            mismatches.append(
                "%s: spec says variadic=%s, but %s:%s declares it NON-variadic"
                "  [%s]" % (e['name'], e['variadic_impl'], sig['header'],
                            sig['line'], sig['qualtype']))
        elif want_variadic:
            # Confirm the v-variant the SPEC named -- never derive it by string
            # munging the export name.  va_list is one ordinary pointer
            # parameter to the oracle, so the v-variant must have exactly one
            # argument more than the variadic has fixed arguments.
            try:
                impl = headers.signature(e['variadic_impl'])
            except Refused as ex:
                mismatches.append("%s: variadic=%s does not resolve: %s"
                                  % (e['name'], e['variadic_impl'], ex.reason))
            else:
                if impl['nargs'] != sig['nargs'] + 1:
                    mismatches.append(
                        "%s: variadic=%s pairing is wrong -- %d fixed args + "
                        "va_list needs %d, but the v-variant takes %d\n"
                        "      %-14s %s  (%s:%s)\n"
                        "      %-14s %s  (%s:%s)"
                        % (e['name'], e['variadic_impl'], sig['nargs'],
                           sig['nargs'] + 1, impl['nargs'],
                           e['name'], sig['qualtype'], sig['header'], sig['line'],
                           e['variadic_impl'], impl['qualtype'],
                           impl['header'], impl['line']))
                elif impl['returns_void'] != sig['returns_void']:
                    mismatches.append(
                        "%s: variadic=%s returns %s but %s returns %s; the "
                        "host returns the v-variant's value as the export's"
                        % (e['name'], e['variadic_impl'],
                           'void' if impl['returns_void'] else 'a value',
                           e['name'],
                           'void' if sig['returns_void'] else 'a value'))
                e['impl_sig'] = impl
        if e.get('asserted', True):
            # The spec's numbers are claims. Wine's headers are the fact.
            if sig['nargs'] != e['nargs']:
                mismatches.append("%s: spec says %d args, %s:%s says %d  [%s]"
                                  % (e['name'], e['nargs'], sig['header'],
                                     sig['line'], sig['nargs'], sig['qualtype']))
            if sig['returns_void'] != e['returns_void']:
                mismatches.append("%s: spec says returns %s, %s:%s says %s  [%s]"
                                  % (e['name'],
                                     'void' if e['returns_void'] else 'int',
                                     sig['header'], sig['line'],
                                     'void' if sig['returns_void'] else 'int',
                                     sig['qualtype']))
            if (sig['header'] != e['header']) or (sig['line'] != e['line']):
                mismatches.append("%s: spec cites %s:%d, clang found it at %s:%s"
                                  % (e['name'], e['header'], e['line'],
                                     sig['header'], sig['line']))
        elif e.get('spec_args') is not None:
            # Derived from a Wine .spec: cross-check clang against the .spec's
            # own argument list.  Two independent readings of the same module
            # have to agree about how many arguments it takes before we publish
            # a descriptor that tells the host how many to marshal.
            if sig['nargs'] != len(e['spec_args']):
                refused.append(
                    (e['name'],
                     "arity disagreement: %s:%d says %d arg(s) (%s), %s:%s says "
                     "%d  [%s]"
                     % (os.path.basename(e.get('spec_path', '.spec')),
                        e['specline'], len(e['spec_args']),
                        ' '.join(e['spec_args']) or 'none',
                        sig['header'], sig['line'], sig['nargs'],
                        sig['qualtype'])))
                continue
        e = dict(e)
        e['sig'] = sig
        e['desc'] = wine_sig.descriptor(sig)
        # What the host resolves in the NATIVE module.  Identical to the export
        # name for everything except a variadic, which forwards to the
        # v-variant the spec named.
        e['impl'] = e.get('variadic_impl') or e['name']
        accepted.append(e)
    if mismatches:
        sys.exit("FATAL: spec disagrees with Wine's headers:\n  "
                 + "\n  ".join(mismatches))

    # An export whose signature came from a Wine .spec rather than a header --
    # the documented last-resort downgrade.  Its `params` are .spec class
    # tokens (ptr, long, str), not C types, so neither the WIDTHS probe nor the
    # FP probe below may be handed them: both compile one translation unit for
    # every request at once and answer for none of them if it fails.
    def spec_cited(e):
        return str(e['sig'].get('header') or '').endswith('.spec')

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Argument WIDTHS, from the same headers that supplied the signatures.
    # An MS-x64 caller stores a 32-bit argument with a 32-bit store and
    # leaves whatever was on the stack in the slot's upper half; PE-side
    # code on this port is LP64, so a native ULONG parameter reads all 64
    # bits of it.  Measured: mspatcha's ApplyPatchToFileByBuffers received
    # a stack-garbage-poisoned buffer size, concluded the caller's buffer
    # was big enough, and the LZXD decoder ran off the end of a guest stack
    # buffer.  So each parameter whose guest-side type is at most 4 bytes
    # gets a bit in the descriptor's narrow-argument mask and the host
    # zero-extends it.  NOT derived from the .spec argument classes: a Wine
    # .spec says `long` for plenty of HANDLEs (HeapAlloc's very first
    # argument), and truncating one of those corrupts every call -- the
    # WineSpecs docstring warns that width is exactly what a .spec cannot
    # state.  Entries the header oracle did not verify (the .spec-cited
    # last-resort path) publish no mask, which is the old behaviour.
    #
    # A .spec-CITED entry MUST BE HELD OUT OF THE PROBE, not merely ignored in
    # the answer -- the same hazard the FP stage below documents, which was
    # fixed there and missed here.  widths() compiles ONE translation unit of
    # `char wt_asz_n_i[sizeof(T)];` for EVERY request together and returns {}
    # for ALL of them if that unit fails to compile.  A .spec-cited signature's
    # `params` are .spec class tokens -- `ptr`, `long`, `str` -- which are not C
    # types, so `sizeof(ptr)` failed the compile and took the whole module's
    # mask with it.  MEASURED, and it was live: kernel32.thunks has always
    # carried two .spec-cited entries (DelayLoadFailureHook,
    # DelayLoadFailureHookLookup), and every one of kernel32's 1142 emitted
    # exports was therefore publishing mask 0 -- HeapAlloc's DWORD flags came
    # out 0x00003 instead of 0x20003, so the host read a 32-bit guest argument
    # slot as 64 bits with stack garbage in the upper half.  That is precisely
    # the mspatcha failure this stage exists to prevent, reintroduced module-
    # wide by one unrelated entry.  Found while adding the four .spec-cited
    # vcruntime140 entries the DOOM triage needs, which would have silently
    # done the same to ucrtbase and vcruntime140.
    widths = headers.widths(
        [(i, e['sig'].get('params')) for i, e in enumerate(accepted)
         if not spec_cited(e)])
    for i, e in enumerate(accepted):
        sizes = widths.get(i)
        if not sizes or len(sizes) != e['sig']['nargs']:
            continue
        mask = sum(1 << j for j, sz in enumerate(sizes) if sz <= 4)
        if mask:
            sig = dict(e['sig'])
            sig['narrow_mask'] = mask
            e['sig'] = sig
            e['desc'] = wine_sig.descriptor(sig)
        # THE SAME MEASUREMENT, KEPT AT FULL RESOLUTION.  The mask above says
        # only "narrower than eight bytes", which the host then reads as
        # "narrow to four" -- and that is wrong for a WORD, a BYTE, a BOOLEAN
        # or a WCHAR.  MS-x64 requires only the low bits of a sub-word
        # argument to be meaningful and real compilers leave the rest alone:
        # clang emits `mov cx, 0xffff` for a WORD, so RCX's upper 48 bits are
        # whatever the last thing to use that register left there.  MEASURED
        # end to end: a guest calling GetActiveProcessorCount(
        # ALL_PROCESSOR_GROUPS) reached native kernel32 as group=0xffbdffff,
        # the `group == ALL_PROCESSOR_GROUPS` test failed, control fell to the
        # else arm and the call answered 0 where Windows answers the processor
        # count.  A wrong number, not a crash, which is the class this port
        # cares about most.
        #
        # Two bits per argument, so sixteen arguments fill one word exactly:
        # 0 = eight bytes (leave it alone), 1 = four, 2 = two, 3 = one.  An
        # unexpected size takes 0 and is left alone, which is what the host did
        # before this word existed.  See THUNK_WIDTH in signal_ppc64.c.
        code = {8: 0, 4: 1, 2: 2, 1: 3}
        e['widths'] = sum(code.get(sz, 0) << (j * 2) for j, sz in enumerate(sizes))

    # AND WHICH OF THOSE SUB-WORD ARGUMENTS ARE SIGNED.
    #
    # Width says how many of the guest's bits are real; it does not say what
    # the rest should become.  ELFv2 makes extension the CALLER's job and ties
    # the kind of extension to the argument's TYPE -- sign for a SHORT, zero
    # for a WORD -- and a ppc64 callee compiled at -O2 is entitled not to
    # re-extend.  Zero-extending everything, which is what this tool published
    # before, turns a SHORT of -1 into 65535 on the way in: the same wrong-
    # number class as the GetActiveProcessorCount case above, and just as
    # invisible to a check that only asks whether the call returned.
    #
    # Only 1- and 2-byte arguments are asked about.  Four-byte ones need
    # nothing whichever way they are signed, because the host narrows them
    # with a cast to UINT and the ppc64 callee re-reads 32 bits; eight-byte
    # ones are not narrowed at all.  Restricting the probe to sub-word types
    # is also what keeps it compilable -- `(ptr)-1 < (ptr)0` is not C.
    #
    # libs/winecom carries this same bit per COM vtable slot
    # (winecom_slot::narrowsign).  This is the flat-surface half of it, and
    # the two were deliberately brought into line rather than left to differ
    # by surface.
    sign_req = []
    for i, e in enumerate(accepted):
        sizes = widths.get(i)
        if not sizes or len(sizes) != e['sig']['nargs'] or spec_cited(e):
            continue
        params = e['sig'].get('params') or []
        sub = [(j, params[j]) for j, sz in enumerate(sizes)
               if sz in (1, 2) and j < len(params)]
        if sub:
            sign_req.append(((i, tuple(j for j, _ in sub)),
                             [t for _, t in sub]))
    for (i, idx), flags in headers.signs(sign_req).items():
        accepted[i]['signs'] = sum(1 << j for j, f in zip(idx, flags) if f)

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Which arguments, and the return, are FLOATING POINT.
    #
    # The two ABIs disagree about where a float travels, so the host has to be
    # told: MS-x64 puts argument i, if floating point, in XMMi -- indexed by
    # POSITION -- while ppc64le ELFv2 puts the n'th floating-point argument in
    # f(n+1), indexed by ORDER.  Neither can be inferred from the arity.
    #
    # Refused rather than described when the shape cannot be carried:
    #   * a floating-point argument past position 3.  MS-x64 has only XMM0-3
    #     for arguments and passes the fifth on the stack, where the host --
    #     which reads its FP arguments out of the guest's XMM registers --
    #     cannot see it.
    #   * more than 8 floating-point arguments, which ELFv2 would start
    #     spilling past f13's window as this port loads it.
    #   * a probe that did not answer at all.  Publishing no FP word for an
    #     export that has floating point would silently hand the host an
    #     integer register file, so it is a refusal, never a default.
    # A .spec-CITED signature has no C types to ask clang about: its `params`
    # are .spec class tokens (ptr, long, str) and its `ret` is the literal
    # "<unverified>" string wine_sig.WineSpecs puts there.  Handing those to the
    # probe produced no answer, and "no answer" is a refusal -- so every export
    # on that documented downgrade path silently stopped being emitted the day
    # the fp stage landed, including the two kernel32.thunks has always named.
    # There is nothing to ask: WineSpecs.BAD_ARGS refuses `float` and `double`
    # on the .spec's own word before this point, so a .spec-cited export that
    # got this far is integer-classed by construction, and its return kind is
    # the human int|void assertion the RETURN KIND NOT VERIFIED banner already
    # prints.  Treat it exactly like an export with no params to probe.
    # (spec_cited() itself is defined above the WIDTHS stage, which needs the
    # same hold-out for the same reason -- see the note there.)

    fp_types = []
    for i, e in enumerate(accepted):
        params = e['sig'].get('params')
        if params is None or spec_cited(e):
            continue
        ret = e['sig'].get('ret')
        fp_types.append((i, list(params) + ([ret] if ret and ret.strip() != 'void' else [])))
    fp_answer = headers.fp_classes(fp_types) if fp_types else {}

    still, newly_refused = [], []
    for i, e in enumerate(accepted):
        params = e['sig'].get('params')
        ret = e['sig'].get('ret')
        has_ret = bool(ret and ret.strip() != 'void')
        if params is None or spec_cited(e):
            still.append(e)
            continue
        nargs = e['sig']['nargs']
        if not params and not has_ret:
            # void f(void): there is nothing to ask about, and the probe is
            # never given an empty request.  Trivially no floating point.
            still.append(e)
            continue
        codes = fp_answer.get(i)
        if codes is None or len(codes) != len(params) + (1 if has_ret else 0):
            # No answer.  Only a problem if something here IS floating point,
            # and without the probe we cannot know -- so refuse only when the
            # acceptance probe already told us a float got through, which it
            # does by the export existing at all.  Be conservative: keep the
            # export only if the oracle saw no FP-classed type, which it
            # cannot report here, so drop it.
            newly_refused.append((e['name'], 'floating-point probe gave no answer'))
            continue
        arg_codes = codes[:len(params)][:nargs]
        ret_code = codes[len(params)] if has_ret else 0
        fp_mask = sum(1 << j for j, c in enumerate(arg_codes) if c)
        if not fp_mask and not ret_code:
            still.append(e)
            continue
        bad = [j for j, c in enumerate(arg_codes) if c and j >= wine_sig.MAX_FP_ARG_POS]
        if bad:
            newly_refused.append((e['name'],
                                  'floating-point argument %d is past XMM3, which '
                                  'MS-x64 passes on the stack' % bad[0]))
            continue
        if bin(fp_mask).count('1') > 8:
            newly_refused.append((e['name'], 'more than 8 floating-point arguments'))
            continue
        if e['sig'].get('variadic'):
            newly_refused.append((e['name'],
                                  'floating point combined with variadic= is not described'))
            continue
        single = sum(1 << j for j, c in enumerate(arg_codes) if c == 2)
        e['fp'] = fp_mask | (single << 8) | (ret_code << 16)
        still.append(e)

    accepted = still
    refused = refused + newly_refused
    return accepted, refused


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# grouping refusals for the report
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A whole .spec refuses hundreds of exports and the useful question is not
# "which ones" -- that is what --report is for -- but "how many, and for what".
# These turn one refusal's message, which names the offending type or export,
# into the CLASS of reason, so the histogram counts causes and not spellings.
# Nothing here decides anything: the refusal has already happened, with the
# compiler's own words, and that text is what goes in the report file.

_REFUSAL_CLASSES = (
    (r'^no declaration found in Wine headers$',
     'no declaration in any Wine header'),
    (r'is not integer/pointer class',
     'argument or return is not integer/pointer class '
     '(struct by value, float, vector)'),
    (r'is wider than 64 bits',
     'argument or return is wider than 64 bits'),
    (r'^varargs ',                       'variadic with no variadic=<v-variant>'),
    (r'^conflicting declarations',       'Wine declares it more than one way'),
    (r'^arity disagreement',             '.spec and header disagree on the argument count'),
    (r'^incompatible pointer types|^initialization of|initializing .* with an '
     r'expression of (incompatible )?type',
     'header prototype does not match the export (reconstruction rejected)'),
    (r'\bdouble argument\b|\bfloat argument\b',
     '.spec declares a float/double argument'),
    (r'\bint128 argument\b',             '.spec declares an int128 argument'),
    (r'descriptor field holds at most 16|host marshaller passes',
     'more arguments than the descriptor or the host marshaller can carry'),
    (r'variadic with no fixed argument', 'variadic with no fixed argument'),
    (r'^unknown \.spec argument type',   'unknown .spec argument type'),
)

_SKIP_CLASSES = (
    (r'^stub ',                  '`@ stub` -- Wine has no implementation'),
    (r'^not exported for ',      'not exported for this machine (-arch=/-i386)'),
    (r'^-noname',                '-noname: no name in the export table'),
    (r'^-register',              '-register: takes a CONTEXT, not arguments'),
    (r'is not a callable entry point', 'data export, not a function'),
    (r'not a plain C identifier', 'name is not a plain C identifier'),
)


def _classify(text, table, fallback):
    for pat, label in table:
        if re.search(pat, text):
            return label
    return fallback


def refusal_class(why):
    return _classify(why, _REFUSAL_CLASSES, 'other (see --report)')


def skip_class(why):
    return _classify(why, _SKIP_CLASSES, 'other')


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# stub bodies
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Exports with a REGISTER contract no trap thunk can carry.  __chkstk's
# MS-x64 contract is rax = frame size in, all argument registers preserved;
# the generic trap stub writes the dispatch result into rax, so a trap
# round-trip would hand the caller's `sub rsp, rax` a garbage frame size and
# corrupt every large-frame function.  Wine's own x86-64 __chkstk is a bare
# `ret` (Windows-side stacks are committed up front, and this port's guest
# stacks likewise -- virtual_alloc_thread_stack), so the correct thunk IS
# that ret, emitted as GUEST code in the stub slot: the call never leaves
# the emulator.  91 of 345 corpus test binaries import it (2026-08-15).
# spec2thunk-check carries the same table for its byte-for-byte stub check.
RAW_BODY_EXPORTS = {
    '__chkstk': b'\xc3',            # ret
}


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GUEST FAST PATHS
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A trap stub is 16 bytes and cannot be anything else: the dispatcher recovers
# an export's index from `(trapping RIP - stubs_rva - trap_off) / stride`, so
# the array's geometry is ABI.  A fast path needs forty-odd bytes and a
# fallback, so it does not live in the slot.  It lives BESIDE the array, and
# the module's export table points the name at it:
#
#   .thunk    [ stub 0 ][ stub 1 ] ... [ stub N-1 ]   <- untouched, still ABI
#             [ fast body for export k ]              <- appended, any length
#                 ...
#             fallback: jmp wine_thunk_stub_k         <- the trap, at its own
#                                                        slot, at trap_off
#   .def      QueryPerformanceCounter = wine_thunk_fast_k
#
# So a call that cannot take the fast path arrives at the trap instruction it
# always did, at the RIP the dispatcher expects, with RCX -- MS-x64 argument 0
# -- still intact, because a fast body must not touch RCX before it commits.
# Everything that resolves a stub by index, GetProcAddress included (the host
# hands back the export table's address, which is now the fast body), keeps
# working; nothing in the host reads a stub address to identify an export.
#
# WHY THIS IS WORTH A MECHANISM.  [MEASURED] 2026-08-27, Cyberpunk 2077's
# -benchmark flythrough: KERNEL32.QueryPerformanceCounter alone was 256,638
# crossings a second, 14,061 a frame, each one a trap AND the syscall the
# native side then made -- the hottest named crossing in the port by a factor
# of 1.5 over the next one.  Windows serves it from user space and so does
# this.  See include/wine/emu_qpc.h for the clock and why it is the same clock
# the native side answers with.
#
# x86-64 ONLY.  The 32-bit lane's stub is a bare `int 0x80` with every
# argument on the stack, and no title reaches it yet (NEXT.md item 2); an
# unproven second copy of this would be a liability, not a feature.
FAST_PATH_MODULES = ('kernel32.dll', 'kernelbase.dll', 'ntdll.dll', 'user32.dll')

FAST_PATH_EXPORTS = {
    'QueryPerformanceCounter':    'qpc',
    'QueryPerformanceFrequency':  'qpf',
    'GetCurrentThreadId':         'tid',
    # The critical-section pair carries BOTH spellings: kernel32's .spec says
    # EnterCriticalSection forwards to NTDLL.RtlEnterCriticalSection, but
    # FROM-SPEC auto gives the kernel32 THUNK its own trap stub under the
    # kernel32 name (the crossing table names the row KERNEL32.dll.Enter-
    # CriticalSection, which is how this was noticed), so each module's
    # export gets its own fast body over the same algorithm.
    'RtlEnterCriticalSection':    'ecs',
    'RtlLeaveCriticalSection':    'lcs',
    'EnterCriticalSection':       'ecs',
    'LeaveCriticalSection':       'lcs',
    'PeekMessageW':               'peek',
}

# Field offsets inside struct emu_qpc_guest.  THE SAME NUMBERS AS
# include/wine/emu_qpc.h -- that header's C_ASSERTs and this table are two
# spellings of one layout, and spec2thunk-check carries a third.
QPC_MULTIPLIER  = 8
QPC_BIAS        = 16
QPC_ENABLED     = 24
QPC_SHIFT       = 25
QPC_CS_DISABLE  = 26
QPC_CS_SABOTAGE = 27
QPC_FREQUENCY   = 32
QPC_TSC_SAMPLE  = 40
QPC_BLOCK_SIZE  = 48
QPC_MAGIC       = 0x5150433150504336   # "QPC1PPC6"


def fast_path_kind(dll, name, mode, machine):
    """Which fast body this export gets, or None."""
    if machine != 'x86_64' or mode != 'trap':
        return None
    if dll.lower() not in FAST_PATH_MODULES:
        return None
    return FAST_PATH_EXPORTS.get(name)


def emit_fast_body(a, kind, idx):
    """The guest x86-64 for one fast-path export, plus its fallback."""
    q = 'wine_thunk_qpc'
    a('\t.globl\twine_thunk_fast_%d' % idx)
    a('wine_thunk_fast_%d:' % idx)
    if kind == 'qpc':
        # BOOL QueryPerformanceCounter( LARGE_INTEGER *counter )   rcx = counter
        #
        # The disarmed leg reads RDTSC anyway and leaves it in tsc_sample: that
        # is how the host learns the emulator's TSC scale, from a guest-visible
        # counter reading it cannot take itself (emu_qpc.h).  The ARMED leg
        # never writes it -- a store there would be one cache line fifteen game
        # threads contend for a quarter of a million times a second.
        a('\tcmpb\t$1, %s+%d(%%rip)' % (q, QPC_ENABLED))
        a('\tjne\t.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d' % idx)
        a('\tmovq\t%rcx, %r10')
        a('\trdtsc')
        a('\tshlq\t$32, %rdx')
        a('\torq\t%rdx, %rax')
        a('\tmulq\t%s+%d(%%rip)' % (q, QPC_MULTIPLIER))
        a('\tmovb\t%s+%d(%%rip), %%cl' % (q, QPC_SHIFT))
        a('\tshrq\t%cl, %rdx')
        a('\taddq\t%s+%d(%%rip), %%rdx' % (q, QPC_BIAS))
        a('\tmovq\t%rdx, (%r10)')
        a('\tmovl\t$1, %eax')
        a('\tretq')
        a('.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d:' % idx)
        a('\trdtsc')
        a('\tshlq\t$32, %rdx')
        a('\torq\t%rdx, %rax')
        a('\tmovq\t%%rax, %s+%d(%%rip)' % (q, QPC_TSC_SAMPLE))
        a('\tjmp\twine_thunk_stub_%d' % idx)
    elif kind == 'tid':
        # DWORD GetCurrentThreadId( void )
        #
        # TEB->ClientId.UniqueThread, which is what Windows' own kernel32
        # export reads and what guest code reads INLINE all over every real
        # binary (gs:[0x48]) -- so unlike QPC there is no host-seeded
        # parameter here, nothing to arm and nothing whose seeding a
        # sabotage lever could break: the value is right whenever guest
        # code can run at all, because the run entry installed this
        # thread's TEB as the GS base before the first instruction.
        # [MEASURED] 2026-08-27, Cyberpunk flythrough: 21,588 crossings/s.
        # The fallback jmp is unreachable; it stays because the checker
        # maps a fast body to its slot through it (spec2thunk-check,
        # order_rva).
        a('\tmovl\t%gs:0x48, %eax')
        a('\tretq')
        a('.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d:' % idx)
        a('\tjmp\twine_thunk_stub_%d' % idx)
    elif kind == 'ecs':
        # RtlEnterCriticalSection( RTL_CRITICAL_SECTION *crit )   rcx = crit
        # (kernel32.EnterCriticalSection is a spec forward to this export)
        #
        # [MEASURED] 2026-08-27, Cyberpunk flythrough: 79,926/s, and every
        # uncontended one has NO syscall behind it -- the native side does one
        # interlocked op and returns.  The trap was the whole cost.
        #
        # THE ALGORITHM IS WINE'S OWN TryEnter, byte for byte in behaviour --
        # it must be, because native RtlEnterCriticalSection runs against the
        # SAME struct from Wine-internal callers, and two lock algorithms on
        # one lock word is a corruption generator:
        #   uncontended:  LockCount -1 -> 0 by cmpxchg, then OwningThread and
        #                 RecursionCount, exactly TryEnter's first arm;
        #   recursion:    owner == tid: interlocked-increment LockCount, then
        #                 RecursionCount++, exactly TryEnter's second arm;
        #   contended:    fall to the trap; the native side spins and waits
        #                 with the semaphore machinery this body cannot have.
        # LOCK CMPXCHG is a full barrier on x86 and stays one under FEX
        # (TSO/HWTSO), so the field stores order exactly as native ones do.
        #
        # RTL_CRITICAL_SECTION x64: LockCount +8, RecursionCount +0xC,
        # OwningThread +0x10.  The tid compares as the full HANDLE: the store
        # zero-extends (ULongToHandle) so the compare must too.
        #
        # cs_disable is the kill switch, cs_sabotage the gate's deliberate
        # wrongness (skip the owner store); both are host-written bytes in
        # the qpc block -- see wine/emu_qpc.h.
        a('\tcmpb\t$0, %s+%d(%%rip)' % (q, QPC_CS_DISABLE))
        a('\tjne\t.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d' % idx)
        a('\tmovl\t$-1, %eax')
        a('\txorl\t%edx, %edx')
        a('\tlock cmpxchgl\t%edx, 8(%rcx)')
        a('\tjne\t.Lwine_thunk_ecs_rec_%d' % idx)
        a('\tmovl\t%gs:0x48, %eax')
        a('\tcmpb\t$0, %s+%d(%%rip)' % (q, QPC_CS_SABOTAGE))
        a('\tjne\t.Lwine_thunk_ecs_sab_%d' % idx)
        a('\tmovq\t%rax, 0x10(%rcx)')
        a('.Lwine_thunk_ecs_sab_%d:' % idx)
        a('\tmovl\t$1, 0xC(%rcx)')
        a('\tretq')
        a('.Lwine_thunk_ecs_rec_%d:' % idx)
        a('\tmovq\t0x10(%rcx), %rdx')
        a('\tmovl\t%gs:0x48, %r8d')
        a('\tcmpq\t%r8, %rdx')
        a('\tjne\t.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d' % idx)
        a('\tlock addl\t$1, 8(%rcx)')
        a('\taddl\t$1, 0xC(%rcx)')
        a('\tretq')
        a('.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d:' % idx)
        a('\tjmp\twine_thunk_stub_%d' % idx)
    elif kind == 'lcs':
        # RtlLeaveCriticalSection( RTL_CRITICAL_SECTION *crit )   rcx = crit
        # (kernel32.LeaveCriticalSection forwards here, like the enter half)
        #
        # [MEASURED] the other 79,926/s.  Only the clean, non-recursive,
        # no-waiter release is fast; everything else is the trap:
        #   RecursionCount != 1        recursion exit -> native;
        #   cmpxchg LockCount 0 -> -1  fails when a waiter has incremented ->
        #                              RESTORE owner and recursion, then trap,
        #                              and the native Leave redoes the release
        #                              properly, wake included.
        # The order is the correctness: owner and recursion are cleared
        # BEFORE the lock word is released (native Leave's own order), and
        # the release is a cmpxchg rather than a decrement precisely so a
        # failed release leaves the lock word HELD and the restore window
        # races nobody -- only the owner writes these fields while the word
        # is held, and we are the owner.  The transient owner=0-while-held
        # state is one native Leave itself passes through.
        a('\tcmpb\t$0, %s+%d(%%rip)' % (q, QPC_CS_DISABLE))
        a('\tjne\t.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d' % idx)
        a('\tcmpl\t$1, 0xC(%rcx)')
        a('\tjne\t.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d' % idx)
        a('\tmovl\t$0, 0xC(%rcx)')
        a('\tmovq\t$0, 0x10(%rcx)')
        a('\txorl\t%eax, %eax')
        a('\tmovl\t$-1, %edx')
        a('\tlock cmpxchgl\t%edx, 8(%rcx)')
        a('\tjne\t.Lwine_thunk_lcs_undo_%d' % idx)
        a('\tretq')
        a('.Lwine_thunk_lcs_undo_%d:' % idx)
        a('\tmovl\t%gs:0x48, %eax')
        a('\tmovq\t%rax, 0x10(%rcx)')
        a('\tmovl\t$1, 0xC(%rcx)')
        a('.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d:' % idx)
        a('\tjmp\twine_thunk_stub_%d' % idx)
    elif kind == 'peek':
        # BOOL PeekMessageW( MSG *msg_out, HWND hwnd, UINT first, UINT last,
        #                    UINT flags )
        #   rcx = msg_out (NEVER touched: the fallback stub needs it intact),
        #   rdx = hwnd, r8d = first, r9d = last, flags at 0x28(%rsp).
        #
        # [MEASURED] 2026-08-27, Cyberpunk flythrough: 100,526/s, each one a
        # full trap AND a full syscall (NtUserPeekMessage) -- the top flat row
        # once QPC fell.  Almost all of them are the render loop's empty poll:
        # PeekMessageW(&msg, 0, 0, 0, PM_REMOVE) with nothing pending.
        #
        # THE EMPTY ANSWER IS A SHARED-MEMORY READ.  Wine's own empty path
        # (dlls/win32u/message.c, check_queue_bits) proves emptiness from the
        # thread's server-maintained queue_shm -- wake/changed masks and bits
        # behind a seqlock -- and returns 0 without any server request.  This
        # body performs THE SAME test against THE SAME shared object, whose
        # address the native side seeded into TEB SystemReserved1[1]
        # (gs:0x198, low bit = the gate's sabotage tag) the first time this
        # thread's real peek looked the queue up (get_shared_queue,
        # dlls/win32u/winstation.c).  Unseeded, every call is the trap it
        # always was.  Only the exact common shape is served: null hwnd,
        # first == last == 0, no PM_QS_* word in flags; QS_SENDMESSAGE is in
        # the tested mask, so a pending sent message always forces the trap.
        #
        # WHAT AN EMPTY NATIVE PEEK DOES THAT THIS BODY DOES NOT, and why
        # each is safe (the analysis behind the design -- keep it current):
        #   check_for_driver_events   the X11 input pump, natively throttled
        #                             to 8 kHz.  Starving it forever would
        #                             kill input, so this body FORCES a real
        #                             peek whenever the KUSER_SHARED_DATA
        #                             tick (0x7ffe0320, wineserver updates it
        #                             every ~16ms) moves, and at latest every
        #                             256 fast answers (a per-thread budget in
        #                             gs:0x1a4, last-tick in gs:0x1a0) --
        #                             the pump runs at poll rate or ~16ms,
        #                             whichever the app's rate gives.
        #   flush_window_surfaces     GDI surface blit; runs on every forced
        #                             real peek, which is the same class of
        #                             throttling the driver check gets.
        #   NtYieldExecution          Wine's own kindness to busy loops, not
        #                             a Windows PeekMessage contract; a loop
        #                             waiting on another thread's post still
        #                             traps the moment the bits show it.
        #   access_time hung-queue    natively, skip only if the server was
        #                             spoken to <3s ago; the forced real peek
        #                             above refreshes it far faster than
        #                             that, so the guard cannot be what
        #                             stands between the app and the server.
        #
        # The seqlock is read exactly as the native reader does: even seq,
        # fields, seq unchanged -- anything else is the trap.  x86 load
        # ordering (TSO, which the emulator preserves) is the fence.
        #
        # queue_shm layout (include/wine/server_protocol.h, shared_object_t):
        #   +0 seq, +8 id, +16 access_time, +24 wake_mask, +28 wake_bits,
        #   +32 changed_mask, +36 changed_bits.
        # Tested constants for the null-filter shape, from include/winuser.h:
        #   wake  0x1cff = QS_ALLINPUT
        #   clear 0x1dbf = QS_INPUT|QS_POSTMESSAGE|QS_ALLPOSTMESSAGE|
        #                  QS_TIMER|QS_PAINT|QS_HOTKEY
        # and both masks must be 0, or the thread's last wait left a state
        # only the server path may touch.
        #
        # The fallback jmp sits FIRST so it lands inside the 96 bytes
        # spec2thunk-check scans for it (order_rva / 8q).
        a('\tmovq\t%gs:0x198, %r10')
        a('\ttestq\t%r10, %r10')
        a('\tjnz\t.Lwine_thunk_peek_on_%d' % idx)
        a('.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d:' % idx)
        a('\tjmp\twine_thunk_stub_%d' % idx)
        a('.Lwine_thunk_peek_on_%d:' % idx)
        a('\ttestq\t%rdx, %rdx')
        a('\tjnz\t.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d' % idx)
        a('\tmovl\t%r8d, %eax')
        a('\torl\t%r9d, %eax')
        a('\tjnz\t.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d' % idx)
        a('\tmovl\t0x28(%rsp), %eax')
        a('\tshrl\t$16, %eax')
        a('\tjnz\t.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d' % idx)
        a('\tbtrq\t$0, %r10')
        a('\tjc\t.Lwine_thunk_peek_empty_%d' % idx)
        a('\tmovl\t0x7ffe0320, %eax')
        a('\tcmpl\t%gs:0x1a0, %eax')
        a('\tjne\t.Lwine_thunk_peek_force_%d' % idx)
        a('\tdecl\t%gs:0x1a4')
        a('\tjns\t.Lwine_thunk_peek_bits_%d' % idx)
        a('.Lwine_thunk_peek_force_%d:' % idx)
        a('\tmovl\t%eax, %gs:0x1a0')
        a('\tmovl\t$255, %gs:0x1a4')
        a('\tjmp\t.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d' % idx)
        a('.Lwine_thunk_peek_bits_%d:' % idx)
        a('\tmovq\t(%r10), %r11')
        a('\ttestb\t$1, %r11b')
        a('\tjnz\t.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d' % idx)
        a('\tmovl\t24(%r10), %eax')
        a('\torl\t32(%r10), %eax')
        a('\tjnz\t.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d' % idx)
        a('\tmovl\t28(%r10), %eax')
        a('\ttestl\t$0x1cff, %eax')
        a('\tjnz\t.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d' % idx)
        a('\tmovl\t36(%r10), %eax')
        a('\ttestl\t$0x1dbf, %eax')
        a('\tjnz\t.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d' % idx)
        a('\tcmpq\t(%r10), %r11')
        a('\tjne\t.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d' % idx)
        a('.Lwine_thunk_peek_empty_%d:' % idx)
        a('\txorl\t%eax, %eax')
        a('\tretq')
    elif kind == 'qpf':
        # BOOL QueryPerformanceFrequency( LARGE_INTEGER *frequency )
        # Free, once the block exists: 299 crossings/s in the same measurement.
        a('\tcmpb\t$1, %s+%d(%%rip)' % (q, QPC_ENABLED))
        a('\tjne\t.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d' % idx)
        a('\tmovq\t%s+%d(%%rip), %%rax' % (q, QPC_FREQUENCY))
        a('\tmovq\t%rax, (%rcx)')
        a('\tmovl\t$1, %eax')
        a('\tretq')
        a('.Lwine_thunk_slow_%d:' % idx)
        a('\tjmp\twine_thunk_stub_%d' % idx)
    else:
        raise ValueError(kind)
    a('')


def stub_bytes(mode, imm, raw_body=None, machine='x86_64'):
    """Exactly STRIDE bytes of guest machine code for one stub slot."""
    if raw_body is not None:
        body = raw_body                                       # RAW_BODY_EXPORTS
    elif mode == 'direct':
        # mov eax,imm32 ; ret -- the same five bytes on both machines.
        body = b'\xb8' + struct.pack('<I', imm) + b'\xc3'
    elif mode == 'trap':
        body = MACHINES[machine]['trap_body']
    else:
        raise ValueError(mode)
    assert len(body) <= STRIDE
    return body + b'\xcc' * (STRIDE - len(body))              # int3 padding


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# assembly / def emission
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# i386 SYMBOL DECORATION
#
# A 32-bit PE object names C symbols with a leading underscore, and lld-link
# resolves /def entries against the decorated name.  Every label this
# generator emits shares the `wine_` prefix and every reference to one is in
# the same translation unit, so decorating the whole listing keeps definition
# and reference in agreement by construction -- which a per-site prefix would
# not.  Export NAMES are untouched: a Win32 DLL exports `D3D11CreateDevice`
# undecorated (this is exactly what a .def is for), and the guest imports it
# by that name whatever the ABI's internal decoration.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

def decorate(text, machine):
    if machine != 'i386':
        return text
    return re.sub(r'\bwine_', '_wine_', text)


def emit_asm(dll, exports, mode, com_ifaces=None, machine='x86_64'):
    out = []
    a = out.append
    a('\t# generated by tools/spec2thunk -- do not edit')
    a('\t# dll=%s body=%s machine=%s stride=%d info_version=%d trap_off=%d'
      % (dll, mode, machine, STRIDE, INFO_VERSION, trap_off(mode, machine)))
    a('')
    a('\t.section\t.thunk,"xr"')
    a('\t.balign\t%d' % STRIDE)
    a('wine_thunk_stubs:')
    for i, e in enumerate(exports):
        b = stub_bytes(mode, e['imm'], e.get('raw_body'), machine)
        a('\t.globl\twine_thunk_stub_%d' % i)
        a('wine_thunk_stub_%d:\t\t# %s' % (i, e['name']))
        a('\t.byte\t' + ','.join('0x%02X' % c for c in b))
    a('wine_thunk_stubs_end:')
    a('')

    # The fast bodies sit past the end of the stub array, in the same
    # executable section.  Nothing indexes them; the export table names them.
    fast = [(i, fast_path_kind(dll, e['name'], mode, machine))
            for i, e in enumerate(exports)]
    fast = [(i, k) for i, k in fast if k]
    if fast:
        a('\t# --- guest fast paths (see FAST_PATH_EXPORTS) ---')
        a('\t.balign\t16')
        for i, k in fast:
            a('\t# %s' % exports[i]['name'])
            emit_fast_body(a, k, i)
    a('\t.section\t.rdata,"dr"')
    a('\t.balign\t8')
    a('\t.globl\twine_thunk_info')
    a('wine_thunk_info:')
    a('\t.long\t%d\t\t\t# version' % INFO_VERSION)
    a('\t.long\t%d\t\t\t# count' % len(exports))
    # With no exports the .thunk section is empty, /opt:noref discards it, and
    # any IMGREL into it is a relocation against a discarded section.  count is
    # 0 there, so nothing indexes the array and 0 is the honest RVA.
    if exports:
        a('\t.long\twine_thunk_stub_0@IMGREL\t# stubs_rva')
    else:
        a('\t.long\t0\t\t\t# stubs_rva  (no exports emitted)')
    a('\t.long\t%d\t\t\t# stride' % STRIDE)
    a('\t.long\twine_thunk_namev@IMGREL\t# names_rva')
    a('\t.long\twine_thunk_sigv@IMGREL\t# sigs_rva')
    a('\t.long\t%d\t\t\t# trap_off' % trap_off(mode, machine))
    a('\t.long\twine_thunk_implv@IMGREL\t# impl_names_rva  (version 4)')
    a('\t.long\twine_thunk_fpv@IMGREL\t# fp_rva  (version 5)')
    a('\t.long\twine_thunk_widthv@IMGREL\t# widths_rva  (version 6)')
    a('\t.long\twine_thunk_signv@IMGREL\t# signs_rva   (version 7)')
    a('')
    # One signedness descriptor per export, ONE bit per argument: set means
    # that argument is a SIGNED sub-word integer and the host must sign-extend
    # it rather than zero-extend it.  Only bits whose width descriptor says 1
    # or 2 bytes can be set; every other bit is zero and the host ignores it.
    #
    # Zero is the safe default and also the old behaviour, so an export the
    # sign oracle could not measure keeps exactly the treatment it had.
    a('\t.balign\t4')
    a('wine_thunk_signv:')
    for e in exports:
        a('\t.long\t0x%08X\t\t# %s' % (e.get('signs', 0), e['name']))
    a('')
    # One argument-width descriptor per export, two bits per argument.  Zero
    # for an export whose arguments are all pointer-width, which is most of
    # them, and zero is also the honest answer for an entry the width oracle
    # could not measure -- that is the behaviour those entries already had.
    a('\t.balign\t4')
    a('wine_thunk_widthv:')
    for e in exports:
        a('\t.long\t0x%08X\t\t# %s' % (e.get('widths', 0), e['name']))
    a('')
    # One floating-point descriptor per export; 0 for the overwhelming
    # majority, which keeps them on the integer path unchanged.  See
    # THUNK_FP_* in ntdll's signal_ppc64.c.
    a('\t.balign\t4')
    a('wine_thunk_fpv:')
    for e in exports:
        a('\t.long\t0x%08X\t\t# %s' % (e.get('fp', 0), e['name']))
    a('')

    a('\t.balign\t4')
    a('wine_thunk_namev:')
    for i in range(len(exports)):
        a('\t.long\twine_thunk_name_%d@IMGREL' % i)
    a('')
    a('\t# The name the host resolves in the NATIVE module.  Identical to')
    a('\t# names_rva[i] -- the same string, deliberately not a copy -- except')
    a('\t# for a variadic, which forwards to its own v-variant.')
    a('\t.balign\t4')
    a('wine_thunk_implv:')
    for i, e in enumerate(exports):
        if e['impl'] == e['name']:
            a('\t.long\twine_thunk_name_%d@IMGREL' % i)
        else:
            a('\t.long\twine_thunk_impl_%d@IMGREL\t# %s -> %s'
              % (i, e['name'], e['impl']))
    a('')
    a('\t.balign\t4')
    a('wine_thunk_sigv:')
    for i, e in enumerate(exports):
        s = e['sig']
        a('\t.long\t0x%08X\t# %d %-24s %d %sarg%s, returns %s  (%s:%s)%s'
          % (e['desc'], i, e['name'], s['nargs'],
             'fixed ' if s.get('variadic') else '',
             '' if s['nargs'] == 1 else 's',
             'void' if s['returns_void'] else s['ret'], s['header'], s['line'],
             (' VARIADIC -> %s' % e['impl']) if s.get('variadic') else ''))
    a('')
    for i, e in enumerate(exports):
        a('wine_thunk_name_%d:' % i)
        a('\t.asciz\t"%s"' % e['name'])
        if e['impl'] != e['name']:
            a('wine_thunk_impl_%d:' % i)
            a('\t.asciz\t"%s"' % e['impl'])
    a('')
    if fast:
        # WRITABLE, and per module and per process by construction: the
        # multiplier the guest needs depends on the emulator's TSC scale, which
        # is this process's bridge configuration, not the machine's.  Linked
        # with enabled = 0, so an image loaded by a host that does not arm it
        # takes the trap on every call -- the behaviour it had before.
        a('\t.section\t.data,"dw"')
        a('\t.balign\t64')
        a('\t.globl\twine_thunk_qpc')
        a('wine_thunk_qpc:')
        a('\t.quad\t0x%016X\t# magic' % QPC_MAGIC)
        a('\t.quad\t0\t\t\t# multiplier')
        a('\t.quad\t0\t\t\t# bias')
        a('\t.byte\t0\t\t\t# enabled')
        a('\t.byte\t0\t\t\t# shift')
        a('\t.byte\t0,0,0,0,0,0\t\t# pad')
        a('\t.quad\t0\t\t\t# frequency')
        a('\t.quad\t0\t\t\t# tsc_sample')
        a('\t.section\t.rdata,"dr"')
        a('')

    if com_ifaces:
        emit_com_asm(a, com_ifaces, machine)
    a('\t# One absolute pointer so the linker is forced to emit a base')
    a('\t# relocation directory; Wine relocates this image.  Pointer-sized,')
    a('\t# so .quad on the 64-bit guest and .long on the 32-bit one -- a')
    a('\t# .quad here would be two i386 relocations, the second against the')
    a('\t# high half of an address that has none.')
    if machine == 'i386':
        a('\t.balign\t4')
        a('wine_thunk_self:')
        a('\t.long\twine_thunk_info')
    else:
        a('\t.balign\t8')
        a('wine_thunk_self:')
        a('\t.quad\twine_thunk_info')
    a('')
    return decorate(  '\n'.join(out) + '\n', machine)


def emit_def(dll, exports, have_com=False, ord_limit=0, machine='x86_64',
             mode='trap'):
    """The .def the thunk PE is linked from.

    Each export carries the ordinal assign_ordinals() gave it, because a guest
    may import by ordinal alone and lld-link's own numbering -- 1..N in NAME
    order -- answers to nothing.  `ord_limit` is the module's whole ordinal
    space; this tool's two info structures are exported past the end of it so
    that an ordinal the thunk cannot serve stays a hole rather than resolving
    to a table of RVAs.
    """
    # NOT decorated, on either machine: lld-link applies the target's own
    # symbol decoration to the names a .def mentions, so spelling the i386
    # underscore here asks it for __wine_thunk_stub_0 -- one underscore more
    # than the object defines.  The OBJECT is decorated (see decorate());
    # this side stays as written.
    sym = lambda n: n
    out = ['LIBRARY %s' % dll, 'EXPORTS']
    have_fast = False
    for i, e in enumerate(exports):
        # A fast-path export's NAME resolves to the fast body; its stub slot
        # stays exactly where it was, and the fast body's fallback jumps to it.
        if fast_path_kind(dll, e['name'], mode, machine):
            target, have_fast = 'wine_thunk_fast_%d' % i, True
        else:
            target = 'wine_thunk_stub_%d' % i
        out.append('    %s = %s%s'
                   % (e['name'], sym(target),
                      ' @%d' % e['ordinal'] if e.get('ordinal') else ''))
    info_ord = ord_limit + 1 if ord_limit else 0
    out.append('    __wine_thunk_info = %s%s DATA'
               % (sym('wine_thunk_info'),
                  ' @%d' % info_ord if info_ord else ''))
    nxt = info_ord + 1
    if have_com:
        out.append('    __wine_com_thunk_info = %s%s DATA'
                   % (sym('wine_com_thunk_info'),
                      ' @%d' % nxt if info_ord else ''))
        nxt += 1
    if have_fast:
        out.append('    __wine_thunk_qpc = %s%s DATA'
                   % (sym('wine_thunk_qpc'),
                      ' @%d' % nxt if info_ord else ''))
    return '\n'.join(out) + '\n'


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# post-link patch: stamp the Wine builtin signature at file offset 0x40
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

def stamp_builtin_signature(path):
    with open(path, 'rb') as f:
        data = bytearray(f.read())
    if data[:2] != b'MZ':
        sys.exit("%s: not an MZ image" % path)
    e_lfanew = struct.unpack_from('<I', data, 0x3c)[0]
    end = SIG_OFF + len(BUILTIN_SIG)          # 0x51
    if e_lfanew <= end:
        sys.exit("FATAL: e_lfanew=0x%x <= 0x%x; stamping the builtin "
                 "signature would clobber the PE header. Refusing."
                 % (e_lfanew, end))
    if len(data) < 96:
        sys.exit("FATAL: image shorter than sizeof(IMAGE_DOS_HEADER)+32")
    data[SIG_OFF:end] = BUILTIN_SIG
    with open(path, 'wb') as f:
        f.write(data)
    return e_lfanew


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

def run(cmd, verbose):
    if verbose:
        print('+ ' + ' '.join(cmd), file=sys.stderr)
    r = subprocess.run(cmd)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        sys.exit("command failed (%d): %s" % (r.returncode, ' '.join(cmd)))


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser()
    ap.add_argument('--spec', help='a .thunks file.  Without a FROM-SPEC line '
                                   'its export lines ARE the list; with one '
                                   'they are overrides for a list taken from '
                                   "the module's own Wine .spec")
    ap.add_argument('--from-spec', metavar='<mod>.spec',
                    help="derive the export list from the module's own Wine "
                         '.spec.  Signatures still come from clang reading '
                         "Wine's headers -- a .spec has no return type and its "
                         '`long` is 32 bits.  May be combined with --spec, in '
                         'which case the .thunks lines are overrides.')
    ap.add_argument('--dll-name', metavar='<name>.dll',
                    help='name for the generated DLL.  Defaults to the DLL line '
                         'of the .thunks file, or to the .spec basename')
    ap.add_argument('--report', metavar='FILE',
                    help='write the machine-readable per-export outcome '
                         '(name, verdict, reason) here, one TSV row each')
    ap.add_argument('--body', choices=('direct', 'trap'), required=True)
    # No default output location: a build tool writes where it is told.  The
    # old defaults pointed into one developer's /tmp and were pure scaffolding.
    ap.add_argument('--outdir', default=None,
                    help='directory for the generated DLL; --out overrides it')
    ap.add_argument('--builddir', default=None,
                    help='directory for intermediates; defaults to a temporary '
                         'one that is removed unless -v is given')
    ap.add_argument('--out', help='explicit output path (overrides --outdir)')
    ap.add_argument('--implib',
                    help='import library path; defaults to '
                         '<outdir>/<stem>.import.lib, which is deliberately '
                         'mode-independent -- the export names are identical '
                         'in direct and trap mode, so one implib serves both')
    ap.add_argument('--wine-include', default=wine_sig.DEFAULT_INCLUDE)
    ap.add_argument('--wine-generated', default=None,
                    help='directory holding the widl-generated headers; '
                         'defaults to ./include then the source include dir')
    ap.add_argument('--wine-source', default=wine_sig.DEFAULT_SOURCE,
                    help='Wine source root, used only to resolve a .spec '
                         'location column (last-resort arity oracle)')
    ap.add_argument('--strict', action='store_true',
                    help='treat any refused export as a fatal error')
    ap.add_argument('--machine', default='x86_64', choices=sorted(MACHINES),
                    help='guest machine to emit for.  The .thunks file '
                         'describes a C surface, not a word size, so the same '
                         'file produces both halves; Wine serves whichever a '
                         'loading guest image needs.')
    ap.add_argument('--clang', default='clang')
    ap.add_argument('--linker', default='lld-link')
    ap.add_argument('--crt-define', action='append', default=None,
                    metavar='-D...',
                    help='override the CRT-selection define(s) the header '
                         'oracle probes with.  By default these are derived '
                         'from the DLL line of the spec by Wine\'s own rule '
                         '(tools/makedep.c get_crt_define): ucrt* -> -D_UCRT, '
                         'msvcrt -> -D_MSVCR_VER=0, msvcr<N> -> '
                         '-D_MSVCR_VER=<N>, anything else -> nothing.  Pass '
                         'this only to probe a runtime other than the one the '
                         'spec names; an empty string means "no define".')
    ap.add_argument('-v', '--verbose', action='store_true')
    args = ap.parse_args()

    if not args.spec and not args.from_spec:
        ap.error('one of --spec <mod>.thunks or --from-spec <mod>.spec is '
                 'required')

    # ---------------- where the export list comes from
    dll, spec_exports, from_spec, excludes = None, [], None, []
    extra = dict(from_def=None, com_json=None, probe_includes=[],
                 probe_extra=[], include_dirs=[])
    if args.spec:
        dll, spec_exports, from_spec, excludes, extra = parse_spec(args.spec)

    def spec_relative(p):
        if os.path.isabs(p) or not args.spec:
            return p
        return os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(args.spec)), p)

    if args.from_spec:
        from_spec = args.from_spec
    elif from_spec == 'auto':
        # `FROM-SPEC auto` = the .spec sitting beside this .thunks file, which
        # is where Wine keeps it.  Named rather than guessed at, so a module
        # that keeps it elsewhere can just say where.
        from_spec = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(args.spec)),
                                 os.path.splitext(os.path.basename(dll or
                                                                   args.spec))[0]
                                 + '.spec')
    elif from_spec and args.spec:
        # A relative FROM-SPEC is relative to the .thunks file that names it.
        if not os.path.isabs(from_spec):
            from_spec = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(args.spec)),
                                     from_spec)

    wine_spec_skipped, wine_spec_dropped, ord_limit = [], [], 0
    if extra['from_def']:
        from_def = spec_relative(extra['from_def'])
        if not os.path.isfile(from_def):
            ap.error('no such .def: %s' % from_def)
        overrides = {e['name']: e for e in spec_exports}
        library, spec_exports, wine_spec_dropped, ord_limit = \
            exports_from_module_def(from_def, overrides, excludes)
        if not dll:
            dll = library or (os.path.splitext(os.path.basename(from_def))[0]
                              + '.dll')
        print("EXPORT LIST  from %s -- a .def carries names ONLY, so every"
              % from_def)
        print("signature below rests on the clang oracle alone; there is no "
              "second reading\nto cross-check.  The oracle still refuses "
              "anything not provably register-passable.")
        print()
    elif from_spec:
        if not os.path.isfile(from_spec):
            ap.error('no such Wine .spec: %s' % from_spec)
        overrides = {e['name']: e for e in spec_exports}
        spec_exports, wine_spec_skipped, wine_spec_dropped, ord_limit = \
            exports_from_wine_spec(from_spec, overrides, excludes)
        for e in spec_exports:
            e.setdefault('spec_path', from_spec)
        if not dll:
            dll = os.path.splitext(os.path.basename(from_spec))[0] + '.dll'
    elif excludes:
        keep = set(excludes)
        wine_spec_dropped = [e['name'] for e in spec_exports if e['name'] in keep]
        spec_exports = [e for e in spec_exports if e['name'] not in keep]
    if args.dll_name:
        dll = args.dll_name
    if not spec_exports:
        sys.exit('no exports to emit')
    names = [e['name'] for e in spec_exports]
    if len(set(names)) != len(names):
        dup = sorted({n for n in names if names.count(n) > 1})
        sys.exit('duplicate export names: %s' % ', '.join(dup))
    for reserved in RESERVED_EXPORTS:
        if reserved in names:
            sys.exit('%s is reserved' % reserved)

    # ---------------- COM interfaces
    com_ifaces = None
    if extra['com_json']:
        if args.body != 'trap':
            sys.exit('COM-JSON requires --body=trap: a COM stub has no imm32 '
                     'sentinel form, only the trap body')
        com_ifaces = load_com_interfaces(spec_relative(extra['com_json']))

    stem = os.path.splitext(os.path.basename(dll))[0]
    tmp_builddir = None
    if not args.builddir:
        tmp_builddir = args.builddir = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix='spec2thunk-')
    os.makedirs(args.builddir, exist_ok=True)

    # Wine's include/msvcrt/ headers describe several different C runtimes out
    # of one tree and default to UCRT's; reading msvcrt.dll's surface in that
    # default mode hides __getmainargs/__set_app_type and substitutes UCRT
    # inline forwarders for the real import declarations of vprintf/_vsnprintf.
    # See the crt_defines_for_dll() banner in wine_sig.py.
    crt_defines = ([d for d in args.crt_define if d]
                   if args.crt_define is not None
                   else wine_sig.crt_defines_for_dll(dll))

    if not args.wine_generated:
        args.wine_generated = wine_sig.find_generated_include()
        if not args.wine_generated:
            ap.error('cannot find the widl-generated headers (wtypes.h); run '
                     'from the top of the build directory or pass '
                     '--wine-generated <builddir>/include')
    probe_src = None
    if extra['probe_includes']:
        probe_src = ''.join('#include <%s>\n' % h
                            for h in extra['probe_includes'])
    probe_extra = ''.join('#include <%s>\n' % h for h in extra['probe_extra'])
    headers = wine_sig.WineHeaders(args.wine_include, args.wine_generated,
                                   clang=args.clang,
                                   workdir=os.path.join(args.builddir, 'sigprobe'),
                                   defines=crt_defines,
                                   probe_src=probe_src,
                                   probe_extra=probe_extra,
                                   extra_includes=[spec_relative(d) for d in
                                                   extra['include_dirs']])
    headers.sanity()
    specs = wine_sig.WineSpecs(args.wine_source)
    t0 = time.time()
    exports, refused = resolve_signatures(spec_exports, headers, specs)
    resolve_secs = time.time() - t0

    unverified = [e for e in exports if e['sig'].get('return_verified') is False]
    if unverified:
        print("!! RETURN KIND NOT VERIFIED -- Wine declares these in no header,")
        print("!! so arity came from the cited .spec line but the int/void")
        print("!! column below is a HUMAN ASSERTION taken from Wine's C source:")
        for e in unverified:
            print("  %-28s %-4s  %s:%d" % (e['name'],
                                           'void' if e['returns_void'] else 'int',
                                           e['sig']['header'], e['sig']['line']))
        print()

    if wine_spec_dropped:
        print("EXCLUDED BY THE .thunks FILE (%d): %s"
              % (len(wine_spec_dropped), ', '.join(sorted(wine_spec_dropped))))
        print()

    if refused:
        print("REFUSED EXPORTS (not emitted -- shape is not representable):")
        # Short lists stay verbatim, exactly as they always printed.  A whole
        # .spec produces hundreds, where a wall of lines hides the answer: what
        # the ceiling on this approach actually is.  So group by reason, biggest
        # first, and show examples -- the full per-export list goes to --report.
        if len(refused) <= 40:
            for name, why in refused:
                print("  %-28s %s" % (name, why))
        else:
            hist = {}
            for name, why in refused:
                hist.setdefault(refusal_class(why), []).append((name, why))
            for cls, rows in sorted(hist.items(), key=lambda kv: -len(kv[1])):
                print("  %5d  %.1f%%  %s"
                      % (len(rows), 100.0 * len(rows) / len(spec_exports), cls))
                for name, why in rows[:2]:
                    print("           e.g. %-26s %s" % (name, why[:150]))
        print()
        if args.strict:
            sys.exit("FATAL: --strict and %d export(s) refused" % len(refused))
    if not exports:
        # An all-refused module is still worth emitting.  Refused exports are
        # absent from the thunk DLL and ntdll binds each to its own named
        # sentinel, so the module's value is that it EXISTS: the guest's import
        # of it resolves, the process reaches its entry point, and only a call
        # to a refused export faults -- with the symbol's name on it.  Refusing
        # to emit turns that into c000007b at load time, which stops the whole
        # process for exports it was never going to call.  msvcp140 and
        # vcruntime140_1 are the honest cases: their exports are C++ mangled
        # names (?...@@) and __CxxFrameHandler4, which no Wine header declares
        # and none ever will, so "every export refused" is their steady state
        # rather than a tooling gap to be fixed later.
        print("WARNING: every export was refused; emitting a module with no "
              "exports.  It will load and every import of it binds to a "
              "sentinel; a call to any of them faults by name.")

    # ---------------- the flat-surface audit (COM-JSON + FROM-SPEC modules)
    # GUEST-REFUSE and GUEST-PASS are CLASSIFICATIONS: the audit below reads
    # them to prove every interface-bearing flat export is accounted for, so
    # they are meaningless without a COM-JSON module to audit against.
    #
    # GUEST-IMPL is not a classification.  It only says "resolve this guest
    # export to that native name", riding the same impl_names_rva plumbing as
    # variadic= -- ntdll learns nothing and no audit consumes it.  It is
    # equally sound on a plain FROM-SPEC module, which is what msvcrt and
    # ucrtbase need for _initterm: the redirect has nothing to do with COM.
    have_guest_directives = extra['guest_refuse'] or extra['guest_pass']
    if have_guest_directives and not (com_ifaces and from_spec):
        sys.exit('GUEST-REFUSE/GUEST-PASS need a FROM-SPEC module '
                 'with COM-JSON: the audit that makes them sound only runs '
                 'there')
    if com_ifaces and from_spec:
        audit_guest_com_flat(exports, spec_relative(extra['com_json']),
                             extra, from_spec)
    elif extra['guest_impl']:
        apply_guest_impl(exports, extra, from_spec or args.spec)

    if wine_spec_skipped or from_spec:
        print("EXPORT LIST  from %s" % (from_spec or args.spec))
        if wine_spec_skipped:
            hist = {}
            for _n, _l, why in wine_spec_skipped:
                hist.setdefault(skip_class(why), 0)
                hist[skip_class(why)] += 1
            print("  %5d  not eligible for an AMD64 thunk at all:"
                  % len(wine_spec_skipped))
            for cls, n in sorted(hist.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1]):
                print("         %5d  %s" % (n, cls))
        print("  %5d  eligible export(s) considered" % len(spec_exports))
        print("  %5d  emitted        (%.1f%% of eligible)"
              % (len(exports), 100.0 * len(exports) / len(spec_exports)))
        print("  %5d  refused        (%.1f%% of eligible)"
              % (len(refused), 100.0 * len(refused) / len(spec_exports)))
        print()

    if args.report:
        with open(args.report, 'w') as f:
            f.write('name\tverdict\tdetail\n')
            for n, l, why in wine_spec_skipped:
                f.write('%s\tskipped\t%s\n' % (n, why))
            for n in wine_spec_dropped:
                f.write('%s\texcluded\tEXCLUDE line in %s\n' % (n, args.spec))
            for e in exports:
                s = e['sig']
                if e.get('fp'):
                    f.write('%s\temitted\tfp=0x%08X %d args, %s, %s:%s\n'
                            % (e['name'], e['fp'], e['sig']['nargs'],
                               'void' if e['sig']['returns_void'] else 'value',
                               os.path.basename(str(e['sig'].get('header'))),
                               e['sig'].get('line')))
                    continue
                f.write('%s\temitted\t%d args, %s, %s:%s\n'
                        % (e['name'], s['nargs'],
                           'void' if s['returns_void'] else 'value',
                           s['header'], s['line']))
            for n, why in refused:
                f.write('%s\trefused\t%s\n' % (n, why.replace('\t', ' ')))

    if not args.out and not args.outdir:
        ap.error('one of --out or --outdir is required')
    outpath = args.out or os.path.join(args.outdir, dll)
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(outpath), exist_ok=True)

    tag = '%s.%s.%s' % (stem, args.body, args.machine)
    spath = os.path.join(args.builddir, tag + '.s')
    opath = os.path.join(args.builddir, tag + '.o')
    dpath = os.path.join(args.builddir, tag + '.def')
    # Opt-in: "make" only wants the DLL, and an unrequested .import.lib beside
    # it is build-tree litter that nothing cleans.  Guest probes that link
    # against a thunk module ask for one explicitly.
    lpath = args.implib
    if lpath: os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(lpath)), exist_ok=True)

    with open(spath, 'w') as f:
        f.write(emit_asm(dll, exports, args.body, com_ifaces, args.machine))
    with open(dpath, 'w') as f:
        f.write(emit_def(dll, exports, have_com=bool(com_ifaces),
                         ord_limit=ord_limit, machine=args.machine,
                         mode=args.body))

    mach = MACHINES[args.machine]
    run([args.clang, '-target', mach['clang_target'], '-c', spath, '-o', opath],
        args.verbose)
    run([args.linker, '/dll', '/machine:' + mach['link_machine'],
         '/nodefaultlib', '/noentry',
         '/opt:noref', '/base:0x%x' % mach['image_base'], '/def:' + dpath]
        + mach.get('link_extra', [])
        + [('/implib:' + lpath) if lpath else '/noimplib',
           '/out:' + outpath, opath],
        args.verbose)

    e_lfanew = stamp_builtin_signature(outpath)

    print("dll          : %s" % dll)
    print("body mode    : %s" % args.body)
    print("crt mode     : %s" % (' '.join(crt_defines) if crt_defines
                                 else '(none -- not a C runtime module)'))
    print("info version : %d" % INFO_VERSION)
    if ord_limit and exports:
        pinned = sum(1 for e in exports if e.get('ordinal_pinned'))
        ords = sorted(e['ordinal'] for e in exports)
        print("ordinals     : %d..%d over an ordinal space of 1..%d "
              "(%d pinned by the export list, %d numbered from %d as "
              "winebuild does); __wine_thunk_info at %d, past the end"
              % (ords[0], ords[-1], ord_limit, pinned, len(ords) - pinned,
                 min(e['ordinal'] for e in exports if e.get('ordinal_pinned'))
                 if pinned else 1, ord_limit + 1))
    elif ord_limit:
        print("ordinals     : no export survived to carry one; the module's "
              "ordinal space is 1..%d and __wine_thunk_info sits at %d"
              % (ord_limit, ord_limit + 1))
    else:
        print("!! ORDINALS   : this export list pins none and derives none, so "
              "the linker")
        print("!! numbers the table 1..%d in NAME order.  A guest that imports "
              "one of these" % len(exports))
        print("!! by ordinal alone binds a sentinel, not a stub.  Only an "
              "export list with")
        print("!! no FROM-SPEC and no FROM-DEF reaches this state.")
    print("output       : %s" % outpath)
    print("e_lfanew     : 0x%x (signature stamped at 0x%02x..0x%02x)"
          % (e_lfanew, SIG_OFF, SIG_OFF + len(BUILTIN_SIG) - 1))
    print("machine      : %s" % args.machine)
    print("trap_off     : %d" % trap_off(args.body, args.machine))
    if com_ifaces:
        nslots = sum(i['slot_count'] for i in com_ifaces)
        print("com mode     : %d interface(s), %d vtable slot(s), %d bytes of "
              "stubs (version %d)"
              % (len(com_ifaces), nslots, nslots * STRIDE, COM_INFO_VERSION))
    print("import lib   : %s" % (lpath or "(none; pass --implib if you need one)"))
    st = headers.stats
    print("oracle       : %d clang run(s) for %d export(s) in %.1fs "
          "(%d AST dump%s indexing %d decls, %d probe compile%s: "
          "%d batched, %d one at a time)"
          % (st['ast_dumps'] + st['probe_compiles'], len(spec_exports),
             resolve_secs, st['ast_dumps'], '' if st['ast_dumps'] == 1 else 's',
             st['indexed_decls'], st['probe_compiles'],
             '' if st['probe_compiles'] == 1 else 's',
             st['batched_probes'], st['fallback_probes']))
    if tmp_builddir and not args.verbose:
        shutil.rmtree(tmp_builddir, ignore_errors=True)

    variadics = [e for e in exports if e['sig'].get('variadic')]
    if variadics:
        print()
        print("VARIADIC EXPORTS -- the host synthesises a va_list from the "
              "guest frame at")
        print("slot <fixed> and calls the impl below instead.  Both signatures "
              "clang-verified:")
        for e in variadics:
            s, im = e['sig'], e['impl_sig']
            print("  %-20s %d fixed  %s:%s  %s"
                  % (e['name'], s['nargs'], s['header'], s['line'], s['qualtype']))
            print("  %-20s -> impl  %s:%s  %s"
                  % ('', im['header'], im['line'], im['qualtype']))
        print()

    # A hand-written list is short enough to read; a whole .spec is 1300 rows of
    # scrollback that nobody reads and that buries the summary above it.  The
    # per-export detail is what --report exists for.
    if len(exports) > 250 and not args.verbose:
        print()
        print("(%d exports emitted; per-export table suppressed -- pass -v, or "
              "--report FILE)" % len(exports))
        return

    print("idx    ord  export                     imm32       desc    args ret   "
          "impl                       from")
    for i, e in enumerate(exports):
        s = e['sig']
        print("%3d  %5s  %-26s 0x%08X  0x%05X %4d %-5s %-26s %s:%s"
              % (i, ('%d*' % e['ordinal']) if e.get('ordinal_pinned')
                 else (e.get('ordinal') or '-'),
                 e['name'], e['imm'], e['desc'], s['nargs'],
                 'void' if s['returns_void'] else 'int',
                 e['impl'] + (' (V)' if s.get('variadic') else ''),
                 s['header'], s['line']))


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
